tel 1.0

base xylophone
import soundness

info   XML as immutable typed values: parsing, literals, derived codecs, namespaces, XPath and schema-driven records
detail
    _Xylophone_ reads and writes [XML](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XML). Text or bytes parse
    into an `Xml` value, a tree whose node kinds are distinct types in the `Xml` companion
    (`Xml.Element`, `Xml.Text`, `Xml.Cdata`, `Xml.Comment`, `Xml.ProcessingInstruction`,
    `Xml.Doctype`, and `Xml.Fragment` for a sequence of nodes with no single parent); a case
    class or enumeration converts to and from XML with the `Encodable in Xml` and `Decodable in
    Xml` derived from its shape; a document's elements are navigated with dynamic member
    access, selected with XPath, or updated through a lens; and XML written literally with
    the `x"…"` interpolator is parsed and checked as the code compiles. A conversion which
    cannot be made raises a typed `Xml.Error` naming the reason, and a malformed document
    raises a `Parse.Error` whose position is a range, not just a line.

    Everything comes from the `soundness` umbrella, with an error strategy and a schema in
    scope. The schema is a choice imported by name — `xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema` admits any
    element and attribute, and an `Xsd` stands in for it when a document should be validated
    against an XML Schema:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

    case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)

    val xml: Xml = t"<Worker><name>Alice</name><age>30</age></Worker>".read[Xml]
    val worker: Worker = xml.as[Worker]                   // Worker(t"Alice", 30)
    val back: Xml = worker.in[Xml]                        // x"<Worker><name>Alice</name><age>30</age></Worker>"
    val text: Text = worker.in[Text over Xml]
    val again: Worker = text.read[Worker in Xml]
    ```

    The pieces, and where to find them:

    - `Xml` is the node type and the home of nearly everything: its companion holds the node
      classes, the `Aggregable`/`Readable`/`Loadable` givens which make `read[Xml]`,
      `read[T in Xml]` and `load[Xml]` work from text or bytes, the primitive and collection
      codecs, the Wisteria derivations for products and sums, the `Showable` and `Formatting`
      which render a tree, the streaming serializers `Xml.emit` and `Xml.lend`, the
      namespace vocabulary (`Xml.Scope`, `Xml.Name`, `Xml.Namespacing`, `Xml.Namespaced` and
      the `@xmlns` and `@unqualified` annotations), the `@attribute` annotation, the XPath
      extensions `select`, `selectText` and `evaluate`, position tracking (`Xml.Position`,
      `Xml.Focus`, `Xml.positionable`, `asTracked`), the direct (tree-free) codec surface
      (`Xml.Parsable`, `Xml.Printable`, `Xml.Field`, `Xml.Reader`, `Xml.Out`, `Xml.Direct`),
      and `Xml.Provider`, a polyvinyl type provider over an XML Schema.
    - `x"…"` and `xp"…"` are the interpolators for XML literals and XPath expressions, each
      checked at compile time; `x"…"` is also a pattern.
    - `XPath` and its package-private engine and parser implement XPath 1.0 over an `Xml`
      tree; `Xsd` is a parsed XML Schema and `XmlSchema` the vocabulary a parse validates
      against; `Namespace` binds a prefix to a URI as a given; `Tag`, `Attributive` and
      `Renderable` are the typed-tag machinery which libraries such as honeycomb and savagery
      build their vocabularies on; `Inlinable` and `inlining` generate direct parsers and
      printers at compile time; `internal` holds the parser, the macros and the opaque
      `Attributes` and `Scope` types which are exported from it.

    Dynamic navigation (`xml.child().grandchild()`) is gated by the `dynamicAccess.dynamicXml`
    given, imported by name, or by a `dynamically[Xml]:` block.

    The library has three source sets:

    - `xylophone.core` — everything above; the parser reads UTF-8 bytes itself, so a byte
      source (a file, an HTTP body, a classpath resource) is parsed as it arrives with no
      `Charset` in scope, and a text source is encoded to UTF-8 first.
    - `xylophone.http` — the `construables`, `postables` and `servables` choice packages, so
      an `Xml` value is an HTTP request or response body with the `application/xml` media
      type and a body parses back to `Xml` on arrival.
    - `xylophone.staged` — `InlinableStager`, the in-macro staging compiler which lets a
      generated direct parser evaluate a user-written `Inlinable` instance at expansion time.
      It is loaded by name from `Inlinable.Stager` when it is on the classpath, and nothing
      refers to it directly.

    The choice packages are givens to import by name, since `import xylophone.*` (and
    `import soundness.*`) does not import givens: `xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema`;
    `formatting.compactXmlFormatting` and `formatting.indentedXmlFormatting`;
    `namespaceOptions.strictNamespaces` and `namespaceOptions.lenientNamespaces`;
    `optionalityOptions.strictXmlAbsence`, `lenientXmlAbsence`, `strictXmlFaults` and
    `lenientXmlFaults`; `optics.xmlLens`, `xmlOrdinalOptical` and `xmlEachOptical` (which
    `lens` finds in implicit scope through their type, so these need no import);
    `dynamicAccess.dynamicXml`; and, from the HTTP component, `construables.xmlConstruable`,
    `construables.textXmlConstruable`, `postables.xmlPostable` and `servables.xmlServable`.
    Each package merges with the umbrella's own package of the same name, so
    `import formatting.indentedXmlFormatting` works after `import soundness.*`. The codec
    modes `codecs.direct`, `codecs.derived` and `codecs.strict` come from distillate and
    select, for every record in scope, whether `read[T in Xml]` and `in[Text over Xml]` use a
    generated tree-free codec, the derived tree codec, or the generated one with a compile
    error where none can be generated.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella except `Attributive`, `Renderable` and
    `Tag`, which clash with honeycomb's names there and are reached as `xylophone.Tag` and so
    on; `attribute` is exported as the type alias `soundness.attribute`, since galilei and
    tarantula already export a term of that name.

refer xylophone.Xml
refer xylophone.x
refer xylophone.xp
refer xylophone.XPath
refer xylophone.Xsd
refer xylophone.XmlSchema
