refer xylophone.Attributes
refer xylophone.Scope

entry .Xml   an XML node or sequence of nodes, and the companion which holds the whole XML vocabulary
  detail
      `Xml` is the sealed type of every XML value: a `Node` — an `Element`, `Text`, `Cdata`,
      `Comment`, `ProcessingInstruction`, `Doctype` or `Header` — or a `Fragment`, a sequence
      of nodes with no single parent, which is what a parse returns (the prolog plus the
      root) and what a selection of several children yields. Every `Xml` is immutable: an
      update returns a new value. A one-node `Fragment` equals its node, so
      `t"<a/>".read[Xml] == Xml.Element(t"a", Attributes.empty, Array())`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml

      val doc: Xml = t"<library><book id=\"1\"><title>Dune</title></book></library>".read[Xml]
      doc.book().title().as[Text]            // t"Dune"
      doc.book(Prim).show                    // t"<book id=\"1\"><title>Dune</title></book>"
      doc.selectText(xp"//book/@id")         // t"1"
      x"<book><title>Dune</title></book>" == doc.book()
      ```

      The type carries three refinements. `Topic` is the label of the node as a literal type
      where it is known (`Xml of "book"`, and `"#text"` for a `Text`); `Transport` is the set
      of labels admissible as its children; `Form` is the `XmlSchema` it was parsed under.
      The typed tags of honeycomb and savagery are built on these; for an `Xml` read from
      text the three are unrefined.

      **Reading.** `text.read[Xml]` and `data.read[Xml]` parse content and drop a leading
      `<?xml …?>` declaration (`.aggregable2`, `.aggregableText2`); `source.load[Xml]` keeps
      the declaration as the `Document[Xml]`'s metadata `Header` (`.loadable`,
      `.loadableData`); `source.read[T in Xml]` parses and decodes in one step
      (`Xml2#aggregableIn`, or the tree-free `Xml1#aggregableParsed` where `T` has an
      `Xml.Parsable`). Each needs an `XmlSchema`, an `Xml.Scope` and an `Xml.Namespacing`
      (the companions give the defaults for the last two) and a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`. A
      byte source is parsed as UTF-8 as it arrives, with no `Charset` in scope.

      **Decoding and encoding.** `xml.as[T]` decodes through the `Decodable in Xml` for `T`:
      the primitives (`.int`, `.boolean`, …), any type with a `Decodable in Text`
      (`.decodable`), collections (`.listDecodable` and relatives), `Optional`
      (`.optionalDecodable`), `Map` (`.mapDecodable`), and products and sums derived by
      `.DecodableDerivation`. `value.in[Xml]` encodes through the mirror givens;
      `value.in[Text over Xml]` and `value.in[Data over Xml]` render straight to text or
      bytes. A failed decode raises `Xml.Error`, and under an accruing strategy
      (`validate[Xml.Focus]`) every bad field is reported at once, each with the `Xml.Focus`
      naming its path.

      **Writing.** `xml.show` renders a node under the `Xml.Formatting` in scope (compact by
      default; `formatting.indentedXmlFormatting` indents element-only content). A whole
      `Document[Xml]` streams out through `.emit` (pull form on a fiber, or push form on the
      caller's thread as text or UTF-8 bytes) or `.lend`, which lends the serializer's own
      buffer block by block.

      **Namespaces.** The parser resolves prefixes as it reads: each `Element` carries the
      bindings in force at it as its `scope`, and `Element#namespace`, `#localName` and
      `#qualified` read the resolved name. Two names are the same when their URIs and local
      parts agree, whatever prefixes bind them, and a prefixed selection (`` doc.`a:x`() ``,
      `elements(Xml.Name(uri, local))`) selects by resolved name. An unbound prefix is a
      parse error unless `namespaceOptions.lenientNamespaces` is in scope. The serializer
      writes the declaration an element's scope needs and nothing above it declared, so a
      subtree built in code or cut from a document writes namespace-well-formed. A derived
      codec puts a type in a namespace with `@xmlns("uri")`, or with an `Xml.Namespaced`
      given for a type which cannot be annotated.

      **Direct codecs.** `Xml.Parsable` and `Xml.Printable` read and write a value with no
      tree built, through an `Xml.Reader` or an `Xml.Out`. A record opts in with
      `given Foo is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable.derived` (or `.staged`,
      `Inlinable.parsable[Foo]`, `Inlinable.printable[Foo]`), with `derives Xml.Direct`, or
      for every record at once with `import codecs.direct`.

      **Schemas.** `object Orders extends Xml.Provider(cp"/xsd/orders.xsd")` reads an XML
      Schema at compile time and `Orders.record(xml)` is a record with one typed member per
      child element and attribute of the schema's root.

      `object Xml` also inherits members from the hidden layers `Xml1`, `Xml2`, `Xml3` and
      `Xml4`, which order their givens by priority: the direct and tree read paths,
      `in[Text over Xml]`, the `Xml.Field` fallback and `Xml.Provider` are declared, and
      documented, there.
  refer xylophone.Xml.Element
  refer xylophone.Xml.Fragment
  refer xylophone.Xml.Error
  refer xylophone.Xml.Scope
  refer xylophone.Xml.Parsable
  refer xylophone.Xml.Printable
  refer xylophone.Xml.Provider
  refer xylophone.x
  refer xylophone.XPath
  refer xylophone.XmlSchema
  entry #Topic   the node's label as a literal type, where it is known; otherwise any `Label`
      `Xml of "book"` is an `Xml` whose `Topic` is `"book"`; an `Xml.Text` has `Topic =
      "#text"`. A node parsed from text is unrefined. The typed tags (`Tag`) refine it so that
      a tag's children can be checked against its `Transport`.
  entry #Transport   the labels admissible as this node's children, as a union of literal types
  entry #Metadata   the metadata of a `Document[Xml]`: its `Xml.Header`
      A `Document[Xml]` from `load[Xml]` pairs a root with a `Header` holding the version,
      encoding and standalone declarations, and the position index when the document was
      loaded with `parsing.trackPositions` in scope.
  entry #Chunks   the chunk type a document streams as: `Text`
  entry #Form   the `XmlSchema` this value was parsed or built under
  entry #as   decodes this value to a type, through its `Decodable in Xml`
      `xml.as[T]` unwraps a one-node `Fragment` and decodes the node. It needs a
      `(T is Decodable in Xml)^` — a primitive given, the `Decodable in Text` bridge, a
      collection or `Optional` given, or the derivation for a case class or enumeration —
      which in turn needs a `Tactic[Xml.Error]`, and a `Foci[Xml.Focus]` (a no-op outside a
      `validate[Xml.Focus]` boundary). A value which cannot be read raises `Xml.Error` with
      the reason: `Malformed` for text which is not the expected type, `Untextual` for an
      element with no single text run, `Missing` or `Absent` for a field with no element.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)

      x"<message>42</message>".as[Int]                                       // 42
      x"<Worker><name>Alice</name><age>30</age></Worker>".as[Worker]         // Worker(t"Alice", 30)
      x"<message>many</message>".as[Int]                                     // raises Xml.Error
      ```

      Positions stay `Unset` on the foci; decode a `Document[Xml]` loaded with
      `parsing.trackPositions` through `asTracked` to have each focus carry its source
      line and column.
  entry #elements   every child element with a resolved name, as a `Fragment`
      `xml.elements(Xml.Name(t"urn:a", t"x"))` is every child, of every element node here,
      whose namespace URI and local part are the name's, whatever prefix the document binds
      it with; `Xml.Name(t"x")` selects children in no namespace. Unlike `selectDynamic`,
      this needs no `Dynamical` and no `Scope`.
  entry #element   the child element at an ordinal with a resolved name, as a one-node `Fragment`
      `xml.element(name)` is the first match and `xml.element(name, Sec)` the second; an
      ordinal with no match gives an empty `Fragment`, not an error.
  entry #selectDynamic   `xml.child`: every child element with the name, as a `Fragment`
      Dynamic navigation, gated by an erased `Xml is Dynamical` — `import
      dynamicAccess.dynamicXml`, or a `dynamically[Xml]:` block — and the `Xml.Scope` in
      scope. `xml.foo` selects every child element labelled `foo` of every element node in
      the fragment, in document order; XML tags are not unique, so the result is a `Fragment`
      of zero or more. A prefixed name (`` xml.`a:x` ``) whose prefix is bound, by the
      `Scope` given or by the parent element's own bindings, selects by resolved name,
      whatever prefix the child uses; an unbound prefix matches the raw label.
  entry #applyDynamic   `xml.child(ordinal)`: one child element with the name, defaulting to the first
      `xml.foo()` is the first child element labelled `foo` and `xml.foo(Sec)` the second,
      each as a one-node `Fragment`, or an empty `Fragment` when there is no such child.
      Gated as `#selectDynamic` is, so the steps chain: `data.b().c().as[Int]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml

      val list = t"<r><x>1</x><x>2</x></r>".read[Xml]
      list.x(Sec).as[Int]    // 2
      list.x.as[List[Int]]   // List(1, 2)
      ```
  entry .Node   the sealed root of the single-node kinds: element, text, CDATA, comment, PI, doctype, header
      A `Node` is one XML node, as opposed to a `Fragment` of several. The kinds are
      `Element`, `Text`, `Cdata`, `Comment`, `ProcessingInstruction`, `Doctype` and `Header`,
      and a match over `Xml` with a case for `Fragment` and one for each is exhaustive. An
      `Element`'s `children` is an `Array[Xml.Node]`. Every node is pure, so one may be held
      anywhere without capturing anything.
  entry .Element   an element: a label, attributes, children, and the namespace bindings in force at it
    detail
        `Xml.Element(label, attributes, children)` is an element whose `label` is the raw
        name as written (`p:a` keeps its prefix), whose `attributes` are an `Attributes` in
        document order, and whose `children` are an `Array[Xml.Node]`. A fourth parameter,
        `scope`, is the `Xml.Scope` of namespace bindings in force at the element — filled by
        the parser, by an `x"…"` literal, or by a derived encoder — which the serializer
        consults to write any declaration the element needs that nothing above it declared.

        `Element` is a plain class, not a case class, because the scope is provenance: it
        takes no part in `equals`, `hashCode` or the extractor, so a parsed element equals
        the same element built by hand, and the pattern `Xml.Element(label, attributes,
        children)` sees the three fields it always did.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        val built = Xml.Element(t"book", Attributes(t"id" -> t"1"), Array(Xml.Text(t"Dune")))
        built == t"<book id=\"1\">Dune</book>".read[Xml]          // true
        built.show                                                 // t"<book id=\"1\">Dune</book>"

        Xml.Element(t"p:a", Attributes.empty, Array(), Xml.Scope(t"p" -> t"urn:p")).show
        // t"<p:a xmlns:p=\"urn:p\"/>"
        ```

        A typed tag's element (`Tag.Container#node`) is an `Element` with `Xml.Vacuiscible`
        mixed in, so it can be applied to children; its attributes are read and written with
        `#selectDynamic` and `#updateDynamic`, each checked against the tag's
        `Xml.XmlAttribute` givens.
    refer xylophone.Xml.Scope
    refer xylophone.Xml.Name
    refer xylophone.Attributes
    entry .apply   makes an element from a label, attributes, children and an optional scope
        `Xml.Element(label, attributes, children)` has an empty scope; pass an `Xml.Scope` as
        the fourth argument for an element whose prefixes (or default namespace) should be
        declared when it is written.
    entry .unapply   the pattern `Xml.Element(label, attributes, children)`: three fields, never the scope
    entry #label   the element's name as written, prefix and all
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, in document order
        An `Attributes`, including any `xmlns` declarations, which are kept as attributes so
        the document writes back as it was read. `attributes(t"id")` is a value (throwing if
        absent), `attributes.fetch(t"id")` an `Optional`.
    entry #children   the child nodes, in order
    entry #scope   the namespace bindings in force at this element, from its ancestors and itself
        The `Xml.Scope` the parser, literal or encoder filled in; empty for an element built
        by hand with no scope given. Not part of equality.
    entry #bindings   the scope extended by any `xmlns` declarations among this element's own attributes
        For a hand-built element with declarations in its attributes but no scope, this is
        where the declarations take effect.
    entry #prefix   the prefix of the label, or `Unset` if the label has no colon
    entry #localName   the label with its prefix removed
    entry #resolve   the URI a prefix is bound to at this element, or `Unset` if unbound
        `element.resolve(Unset)` is the default namespace. A prefix bound by the element's
        own attributes resolves, as does one bound by an ancestor.
    entry #namespace   the URI of this element's own name, or `Unset` if it is in no namespace
    entry #qualified   the resolved name: namespace URI and local part, as an `Xml.Name`
        The accessor is `qualified`, not `name`, because the typed tags are elements and
        `Format` already gives them a `name`. Two elements whose `qualified` names are equal
        are the same element name whatever prefixes they use; shown in Clark notation,
        `{urn:a}x`.
    entry #attribute   the value of the attribute with a resolved name, or `Unset`
        `element.attribute(Xml.Name(t"urn:a", t"id"))` finds an attribute whose prefix
        resolves to the URI and whose local part matches; `Xml.Name(t"id")` finds the
        unprefixed attribute `id`. An unprefixed attribute is in no namespace, whatever the
        default namespace is.
    entry #selectDynamic   `element.attr`: an attribute's value, checked against the tag's attribute givens
        For typed tags: `element.href` reads the `href` attribute, provided an
        `"href" is Xml.XmlAttribute on element.Topic in element.Form` is in scope, so a
        misspelt or inapplicable attribute is a compile error. The result is an
        `Optional[Text]`.
    entry #updateDynamic   `element.attr = value`: a copy of the element with the attribute set
        For typed tags: returns a new `Element` of the same `Topic`, `Transport` and `Form`
        with the attribute updated, checked against the tag's `Xml.XmlAttribute` givens.
  entry .Text   a run of character data
    detail
        `Xml.Text(t"hello")` is a text node; its `Topic` is `"#text"`. Entities are resolved
        on the way in (`&amp;` is the character `&` in `text`) and re-escaped on the way out;
        a carriage return is written as `&#xD;` so line-ending normalisation cannot rewrite
        it. Text and `String` values convert to an `Xml.Text` implicitly in a typed tag's
        children (`.text`, `.string`).
    entry #text   the characters, with entities resolved
    entry #Topic   `"#text"`
  entry .Cdata   a `<![CDATA[…]]>` section, kept as a section
    detail
        Text which contains `<not a tag>` survives a round trip without being escaped into
        something else. A CDATA section is not text for the purposes of decoding: an element
        whose only child is a `Cdata` has no single text run, so `as[Int]` on it raises
        `Xml.Error` with `Untextual`; the schema provider's readers concatenate text and
        CDATA alike.
    entry #text   the content between the delimiters, verbatim
  entry .Comment   a `<!-- … -->` comment, wherever it appears
    detail
        Kept in the prolog and between elements alike, and written back verbatim. A comment
        between the children a derived decoder expects is ignored.
    entry #text   the comment's content
  entry .ProcessingInstruction   a `<?target data?>` instruction, including the empty-data `<?target?>`
    detail
        A stylesheet instruction in a prolog survives a read and a write. The target may not
        be `xml` in any case, which is reserved for the declaration.
    entry #target   the instruction's target
    entry #data   the instruction's data, or empty text for `<?target?>`
  entry .Doctype   a `<!DOCTYPE …>` declaration, which serialises back verbatim
    detail
        The parser keeps the declaration's text; it has no DTD internal subset support, so a
        declaration with one is not parsed.
    entry #text   the declaration's content after `DOCTYPE`
  entry .Header   the `<?xml …?>` declaration: version, encoding, standalone, and the position index
    detail
        The metadata of a `Document[Xml]`: `load[Xml]` fills it from the declaration (and
        `Xml.header` is the plain `1.0` header for a document which had none), and `show`
        and `emit` write it back. `positionIndex` is present when the document was loaded with
        `parsing.trackPositions` in scope, and is excluded from `equals` and `hashCode`, so a
        tracked and an untracked load of the same source compare equal.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
        import threads.platformThreads

        supervise:
          t"""<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><root/>""".load[Xml].metadata
          // Xml.Header(t"1.0", t"UTF-8", Unset)
        ```
    entry #version   the XML version, usually `1.0`
    entry #encoding   the declared encoding, or `Unset`
    entry #standalone   the `standalone` declaration, or `Unset`
    entry #positionIndex   the source-position index recorded by a tracked load, or `Unset`
  entry .Fragment   a sequence of nodes with no single parent
    detail
        What a parse returns (the prolog's nodes plus the root), what a dynamic selection
        returns (zero or more matching children), and what an `Optional` or collection
        encoder produces before the product encoder flattens it into repeated children. A
        `Fragment` of one node equals that node, and an empty `Fragment` is the absent
        sentinel the decoders hand to a missing field.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val fragment: Xml = Xml.Fragment(Xml.Comment(t"note"), Xml.Element(t"a", Attributes.empty, Array()))
        fragment.show   // t"<!--note--><a/>"
        ```
    entry .apply   a fragment of nodes, with the label of the whole as a type argument
        `Xml.Fragment[topic](nodes*)` accepts any `Xml` values whose `Topic` is within
        `topic`, flattens nested fragments into their nodes, and types the result as
        `Fragment of topic`; the typed tags use it for a tag's children.
    entry #nodes   the nodes, in order
  entry .Vacuiscible   lets a typed tag's element be applied to children
    detail
        Mixed into the element a `Tag.Container` produces, so that `div(id = t"x")(p(t"hi"))`
        reads as a tag applied to attributes, then to children. Each child is an
        `Optional[Xml of (? <: node.Transport)]`, so an `Unset` child is dropped and a child
        of an inadmissible label is a compile error.
    entry #apply   a copy of the element with the given children, `Unset` ones dropped
  entry .Error   raised when XML cannot be read as the type asked for
    detail
        Error 149: _the XML could not be read because `reason`_, with a reason from
        `Xml.Error.Reason`. Raised by the primitive decoders, the text bridge, the derived
        product and sum decoders, and the direct parsers. These are raise-and-continue
        errors: a decoder records the error through its `Tactic[Xml.Error]` and continues
        with a zero or empty sentinel, so a record with several bad fields accrues one error
        per field under `validate[Xml.Focus]` rather than stopping at the first, and each
        error's `Xml.Focus` carries the XPath of the field at fault. Under
        `strategies.throwUnsafely` the first is thrown.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)

        val attempt = validate[Xml.Focus]:
          x"<Worker><name>Ann</name><age>many</age></Worker>".as[Worker]
        ```
    refer xylophone.Xml.Focus
    entry .Reason   why the XML could not be read, each with its number
        The cases, each extending `Reason(n)` so the error reads as `SN-149.n`:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `Malformed(text, expected)`  1  _the text `text` is not a valid `expected`_
        row  `Untextual(expected)`  2  _the element has no text to read as `expected`_  no single text run
        row  `Absent(expected)`  3  _no element or attribute supplied the required `expected`_  a required primitive
        row  `AbsentProduct(product)`  4  _no element supplied the required `product`, which has no default_  a nested record
        row  `AbsentVariant(sum)`  5  _no element supplied the required `sum`, which has no default_  an enumeration
        row  `UnknownVariant(discriminant, sum)`  6  _`discriminant` does not name a variant of `sum`_  an element label
        row  `Empty`  7  _the element has no text_  where the text bridge needed some
        row  `Missing`  8  _the element or attribute was not present_  where the type cannot be named at the raise site
        ```
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   the `Xml.Error.Reason`
  entry .Issue   what the parser or the `x"…"` macro found wrong with XML text
    detail
        The issues a `Parse.Error` for XML carries, each with a `describe` message:
        `BadInsertion` (a value cannot be interpolated at this point of a literal),
        `ExpectedMore` (the content ended prematurely), `BadDocument` (no single root tag),
        `UnquotedAttribute`, `InvalidTag(name)`, `InvalidTagStart(prefix)`,
        `DuplicateAttribute(name)`, `InadmissibleTag(name, parent)` (the schema does not
        allow the child under the parent), `OnlyWhitespace(char)`, `Unexpected(char)`,
        `UnknownEntity(name)`, `ForbiddenUnquoted(char)`, `MismatchedTag(open, close)`,
        `UnopenedTag(close)`, `Incomplete(tag)` (the content ended with the tag open),
        `UnknownAttribute(name)`, `UnknownAttributeStart(name)`,
        `InvalidAttributeUse(attribute, element)`, `UnboundPrefix(prefix)` (a prefix with no
        namespace binding, under `Xml.Namespacing.Strict`) and `BadEncoding` (the input is
        not valid UTF-8). The `Parse.Error`'s position is a range — the offset and length of
        the text at fault, in bytes for a byte source and characters for a whole `Text` —
        so a tool can underline exactly the mismatched closing tag.
    entry #describe   the issue as a message
  entry .Position   a source position: line, column, and the byte offset and length of a span
    detail
        Where a tracked document's element or a parse error lies. `line` and `column` are
        ordinals (`Prim` is the first); `offset` and `length` count bytes for a byte source,
        which is what an editor needs to underline a span in a UTF-8 file, and equal the
        character counts for ASCII. `Document[Xml]#locate(xpath)` resolves an `XPath` to one
        for a document loaded with `parsing.trackPositions` in scope.
    entry #line   the line, as an ordinal
    entry #column   the column, as an ordinal, counting code points
    entry #offset   the byte offset into the source, or `Unset`
    entry #length   the length of the span, or `Unset`
    entry #describe   `line 3, column 7`
    entry #span   the position as a `Span` for a diagnostic
  entry .PositionIndex   the compact record of where every element of a tracked document lies
      An opaque array of integers built by a tracked load and held in the document's
      `Header`. It has no public operations of its own: `Document[Xml]#locate(xpath)`
      (through `.positionable`) and `asTracked` read it.
  entry .Focus   where in a document a decoding error occurred: the XPath, and the position if tracked
    detail
        The focus type of XML decoding: `validate[Xml.Focus]` collects every `Xml.Error`
        raised while decoding a document together with the `XPath` of the field at fault.
        `position` is `Unset` unless the document was loaded with `parsing.trackPositions`
        and decoded through `asTracked`, which looks each path up in the position index.
    entry #path   the XPath to the node being decoded
    entry #position   the source position, or `Unset` for an untracked document
    entry #withPosition   a copy with `position` resolved against a tracked document
  entry .positionable   resolves an `XPath` to an `Xml.Position` in a tracked `Document[Xml]`
      The `Positionable` given behind `document.locate(path)`: `Unset` for a document loaded
      without `parsing.trackPositions`, or for a path which matches nothing. The path's
      first step names the root element itself; `locateKey` is always `Unset`, since XML has
      no key positions distinct from its elements.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import parsing.trackPositions

      val tracked: Document[Xml] = t"<root><child/></root>".load[Xml]
      tracked.locate(XPath().element(t"root", 1).element(t"child", 1)).let(_.column.n1)   // 7
      ```
  entry .asTracked   decodes a tracked document, giving every accrued focus its source position
      An extension on `Document[Xml]`. `document.asTracked[T]` decodes like `Xml#as`, then
      supplements each `Xml.Focus` accumulated inside the surrounding `validate[Xml.Focus]`
      with the position its XPath resolves to in the document's index, so a validation
      report can name line and column. Outside a `validate` block the foci are inert and the
      call costs nothing; for an untracked document the positions stay `Unset`. Named
      `asTracked` rather than `as` because `as` is the generic decoder extension.
  entry .aggregable   `source.read[Xml of content]` from bytes: parses a typed fragment, dropping any declaration
      The `Aggregable by Data` given for an `Xml of content` whose `content` label is
      reifiable. Needs an `XmlSchema`, an `Xml.Scope`, an `Xml.Namespacing`, a
      `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and a `Buffering`. A stream is parsed as it arrives, not
      gathered first; a leading `<?xml …?>` declaration is accepted and dropped, so a whole
      document reads as a fragment (use `load[Xml]` to keep it).
  entry .aggregable2   `source.read[Xml]` from bytes: parses content, dropping any declaration
      The plain-`Xml` form of `.aggregable`, with the same contextual needs. The parser reads
      UTF-8 bytes directly, so a file, an HTTP body or a classpath resource is parsed with
      no `Charset` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      t"<message>hello world</message>".read[Xml]
      ```
  entry .aggregableText   `text.read[Xml of content]`: the text is encoded to UTF-8 and parsed as bytes
  entry .aggregableText2   `text.read[Xml]`: the text is encoded to UTF-8 and parsed as bytes
      A `Text` source costs one copy, to UTF-8; a `Parse.Error`'s offset and length for a
      whole `Text` are converted back to character counts, so a literal's error span is
      right for non-ASCII content.
  entry .loadable   `text.load[Xml]`: a `Document[Xml]` with its `<?xml …?>` header kept as metadata
      The `Loadable by Text` given. The root is parsed as `.aggregableText2` would parse it,
      but a leading declaration is lifted into the document's `Xml.Header` rather than
      dropped, and a document with none gets `Xml.header`. Needs the parse context
      (`XmlSchema`, `Xml.Scope`, `Xml.Namespacing`, `Tactic[Parse.Error]`) and a
      `PositionTracking`: with `parsing.trackPositions` in scope the parser records a
      `PositionIndex` in the header, which `Document[Xml]#locate` and `asTracked` read.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import threads.platformThreads

      supervise:
        t"""<?xml version="1.0"?><root>content</root>""".load[Xml]
        // Document(x"<root>content</root>", Xml.Header(t"1.0", Unset, Unset))
      ```
  entry .loadableData   `data.load[Xml]` or `path.load[Xml]`: a document from a byte source, parsed as it arrives
      As `.loadable`, from bytes: a file, a classpath resource or an HTTP body loads with no
      `Charset`, and positions count bytes.
  entry .streamable   a `Document[Xml]` streams as `Text`, serialised on a fiber
      The `Streamable by Text over Credit` given, built on the pull form of `.emit`: needs a
      `Monitor` and a `Probate`, so it is used under `supervise`. `document.read[Text]`
      renders the whole document, header and all, under the `Xml.Formatting` in scope.
  entry .abstractable   an `Xml` value as an HTTP body: `application/xml` with the `Codepage`'s charset
      The `Abstractable across HttpStreams` given, from which telekinesis derives a
      `Postable` and a `Servable`; the body is the value's `show`, encoded in the `Codepage`
      in scope. The `postables.xmlPostable` and `servables.xmlServable` givens of
      `xylophone.http` are the direct forms.
  entry .instantiable   an HTTP request or response body, read back as `Xml`
      The `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text` given: parses the body's text with
      the parse context in scope, raising `Parse.Error` for malformed XML.
  entry .showable   renders any `Xml` as text, under the `Xml.Formatting` in scope
      `xml.show` renders a node, fragment or header to one `Text`: compact on a single line
      by default, or indented when `formatting.indentedXmlFormatting` is imported. Character
      data is escaped (`&`, `<`, `>`, and `\r` as `&#xD;`), attribute values additionally
      for `"`, tab and newline, and the content of comments, CDATA sections, doctypes and
      processing instructions is written verbatim. An element whose scope binds a prefix it
      uses which no ancestor declared gets the `xmlns` declaration written for it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val worker = Xml.Element(t"Worker", Attributes.empty, Array(Xml.Element(t"name", Attributes.empty, Array(Xml.Text(t"Alice")))))

      locally:
        import formatting.compactXmlFormatting
        worker.show   // t"<Worker><name>Alice</name></Worker>"

      locally:
        import formatting.indentedXmlFormatting
        worker.show   // t"<Worker>\n  <name>Alice</name>\n</Worker>\n"
      ```

      An element with character data among its children is always written inline, so
      indentation can never alter its content.
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering as an `xml"…"` literal, compact and on one line
      Fixes compact formatting, since a debugger cannot supply a `Formatting`, and renders
      the node's own source escaped into a literal: `xml"<message>hello</message>"`, which is
      distinguishable from the `Text` holding the same markup and from honeycomb's
      `html"…"`.
  entry .Formatting   how a tree is serialised: the indent unit, if any, and whether a newline ends it
    detail
        `indent` is the whitespace emitted per nesting level when indenting element-only
        content, or `Unset` to keep everything on one line; `trailingNewline` appends a final
        newline. The companion's `default` is compact, and the `formatting` package bundles
        `compactXmlFormatting` (the same) and `indentedXmlFormatting` (two spaces, with a
        trailing newline), to import by name. Every renderer — `show`, `emit`, `lend`, the
        `Streamable`, and `in[Text over Xml]` — takes the `Xml.Formatting` in scope.
    entry .apply   a formatting from an optional indent unit and the trailing-newline flag
    entry .default   compact: no indent, no trailing newline
    entry #indent   the indent unit, or `Unset` for single-line output
    entry #trailingNewline   whether a newline ends the output
  entry .emit   serialises a `Document[Xml]` in blocks: pulled through an iterator, or pushed to a function
      Two forms. `Xml.emit(document)` serialises on a fiber and hands out the text through
      an `Iterator[Text]`, so a large document is written to a socket or file before it is
      fully rendered; it needs an `Xml.Formatting`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate`, so it runs
      under `supervise`. `Xml.emit[medium](document, deliver)` is the push form: it
      serialises on the caller's thread and hands each block to `deliver` as it fills, with
      no fiber and no thread handoff per block — the right shape for writing to a file, a
      socket or an `OutputStream`. The medium is chosen by the type argument through an
      `Xml.Emitter`: `emit[Text]` delivers text blocks, and `emit[Data]` delivers UTF-8
      bytes escaped and encoded straight from the serialiser, with no intermediate `Text`.
      Both take an `Xml.Formatting`, and the push form a `Buffering` for the block size.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Xml.emit[Data](document, chunk => socket.write(chunk))

      supervise:
        Xml.emit(document).each(chunk => out.write(chunk))
      ```
  entry .lend   serialises a document to UTF-8 and lends each filled block, copying nothing
      The borrowing form of the push `emit`: the consumer sees the writer's own buffer as a
      `Region[Data]` with a branded interval proving every index in range, valid only for
      the duration of the call — the discipline of `Stream.lend`. A consumer which must keep
      the bytes materialises them itself; `emit[Data]` is that consumer for the common
      case. Needs an `Xml.Formatting` and a `Buffering`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Xml.lend(document): region =>
        interval => region.visit(interval)(index => sink.put(region(index)))
      ```
  entry .Emitter   how the push form of `emit` reaches its consumer: as text blocks or as UTF-8 bytes
    detail
        A typeclass over the medium: `Emitter[Text]` writes through a `Producer[Text]`, and
        `Emitter[Data]` through the byte-level UTF-8 writer behind `lend`, materialising each
        block. Both givens are in the companion, so `emit[Text]` and `emit[Data]` need no
        import.
    entry .text   the text medium: blocks of `Text`
    entry .data   the byte medium: blocks of UTF-8 `Data`, encoded directly from the serialiser
    entry #emit   writes a document under a formatting, handing each block to a function
  entry .Out   the output an `Xml.Printable` writes into: five leaf operations, one per kind of markup
    detail
        An exclusive, stateful capability which the serialiser and generated printers write
        through, so one traversal serves text output (`show`, the pull `emit`) and byte
        output (`lend`, `emit[Data]`) alike. Its implementations are internal; a hand-written
        `Xml.Printable` calls these methods, or the helpers on `Xml.Printable` such as
        `writeLeaf`.
    entry #ascii   markup known to be ASCII: delimiters and keywords
    entry #name   an element or attribute name, or a prefix, written verbatim
        A document repeats its names, so a byte-level output may cache their encodings.
    entry #raw   text written verbatim, escaped for nothing: comments, CDATA, doctypes and PIs
    entry #text   character data, escaped for text content
    entry #attribute   an attribute value, escaped for a double-quoted attribute
  entry .header   the default header, `<?xml version="1.0"?>`, for a document read without one
  entry .name   the format's name, `XML`, as a `Format`
  entry .Topic   the companion's own topic, `"xml"`, as a `Tag.Container`
      `object Xml` is itself a `Tag.Container` labelled `xml` admitting `head` and `body`,
      which is how the typed tags' machinery reaches a root; this is not the `Topic` of an
      `Xml` value, which is the instance member.
  entry .Transport   the companion's own admissible children as a container: `"head" | "body"`
  entry .Integral   an unused marker trait
  entry .Decimal   an unused marker trait
  entry .Id   an unused marker trait
  entry .nodes   flattens a `List[Xml]` into one `Array[Xml.Node]`, splicing fragments
      An extension on `List[Xml]`: each `Fragment` contributes its nodes and each node
      itself, so a list of children built from mixed values becomes the array an
      `Xml.Element` takes.
  entry .text   converts a `Text` to an `Xml.Text`, for a typed tag's children
      A `Conversion[Text, Xml of label]` for any label admitting `"#text"`, so `p(t"hello")`
      works on a typed tag. Not for general use: it is found through the companion only
      where an `Xml of label` is expected.
  entry .string   converts a `String` to an `Xml.Text`, as `.text` does
  entry .conversion3   widens an `Xml of label` to an `Xml of content` where the label is within the content
  entry .comment   admits an `Xml.Comment` as the child of any typed tag
  entry .xmlConversion   converts any `Encodable in Xml` value to `Xml`, where an `Xml` is expected
      The coercion behind `document.lens(_.Worker = Worker(t"Alice", 30))`: the right-hand
      side of a lens assignment, or any `Xml` parameter, accepts a case class, an `Int` or a
      `Text` and encodes it. Other formats ask for an import such as
      `conversions.encodableToJson`; XML's lives on the companion.
  entry .sequences   lifts a conversion over the elements of a `Seq`
  entry .interpolator   the `x"…"` interpolator's implementation: parses and checks a literal as it compiles
      The `Interpolable` given which the `x` extension on `StringContext` uses. Its macro
      parses the literal's parts leniently, checks every prefix the literal uses against
      the `Namespace` givens in scope (an undeclared, unbound prefix is a compile error),
      reports a malformed literal at the exact span at fault, and substitutes each hole by
      its position: a `Text` or any `Encodable in Xml` as content, an `Xml` as a node, a
      `Text` as an attribute value. See `x`.
  entry .extrapolator   the `x"…"` pattern's implementation: matches a literal shape and binds its holes
      The `Extrapolable` given behind `case x"<a id=$value>$child</a>"`. See `x` for the
      shapes a pattern may take and what each hole binds.
  entry .Hole   the positions an `x"…"` literal or pattern may have a hole in
      `Text` (character data), `Tagbody` (inside a start tag, for attributes), `Comment`,
      `Element(tag)` (the whole element), `Attribute(tag, attribute)` (an attribute's value)
      and `Node(parent)` (a child node). The interpolator macro classifies each `$` by the
      context it lands in and rejects one in a position with no meaning, such as inside
      CDATA, with `Xml.Issue.BadInsertion`.
  entry .select   evaluates an XPath against this tree and returns the matching nodes as a `Fragment`
      An extension on `Xml`. `xml.select(xp"//book[@id='1']")` is a `Fragment` of the
      matching tree nodes in document order. A path selecting attributes (`//a/@href`)
      yields nothing here, since attributes are not nodes: read their values with
      `selectText` or `evaluate`. An expression whose value is not a node set raises
      `XPath.Error` with `NotNodeSet`; an expression the engine does not support, or a
      variable it was not given, raises `XPath.Error` too. Prefixed name tests resolve
      through the path's own scope (`XPath#in(scope)`, or the `Namespace` givens an
      `xp"…"` literal was written under).
  entry .selectText   the string value of the first matching node, or of a scalar expression
      `xml.selectText(xp"//a/@href")` is the attribute's value, `Unset` when nothing
      matches; for a number, boolean or string expression it is the value's text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      val page = t"""<app><div id="main"><button data-test="submit">Submit</button><ul><li>one</li><li>two</li></ul></div></app>""".read[Xml]
      page.selectText(xp"//button[text()='Submit']/@data-test")   // t"submit"
      page.selectText(xp"/app/div[1]/ul/li[2]")                    // t"two"
      page.evaluate(xp"count(//li)")                               // XPath.Value.Numeric(2)
      ```
  entry .evaluate   full XPath 1.0 evaluation: a number, boolean, string or node set, as an `XPath.Value`
      `xml.evaluate(xpath, variables)` resolves `$name` references from the map, keyed by
      qualified name, and raises `XPath.Error` for an unknown variable or an unsupported
      expression.
  entry .Scope   the namespace bindings in force at a point: prefix to URI, with the default keyed by the empty prefix
    detail
        `Xml.Scope` is the opaque `xylophone.internal.Scope`, reached here because the
        umbrella already exports a `Scope` of its own (it is also exported as
        `xylophone.Scope`). A scope is an ordered list of bindings from a prefix (`Unset`,
        or the empty text, for the default namespace) to a URI, the later binding of a
        prefix winning. Every `Xml.Element` carries one as its `scope`; the parser pushes
        each `xmlns` declaration onto the scope of the element and its descendants; and the
        `Scope` given in scope is the one a document is parsed in and a prefixed selection is
        resolved against. The companion's `default` binds only the reserved `xml` prefix.

        A scope bound by a given lets code select by a prefix the document never used:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
        import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml

        val doc = t"""<r xmlns:a="urn:a"><a:x>1</a:x></r>""".read[Xml]

        locally:
          given Xml.Scope = Xml.Scope(t"q" -> t"urn:a")
          doc.`q:x`().as[Int]   // 1: q and a bind the same URI
        ```

        A scope given also binds a prefix for parsing, so `<q:x/>` parses without a
        declaration while the given is in scope, and an element built with a scope declares
        what it needs when written. An `Xml.Scope[("svg", "xlink")]` is the scope binding
        each prefix of the tuple through its `Namespace` given.
    refer xylophone.Namespace
    refer xylophone.Xml.Name
  entry .Name   a resolved name: the namespace URI, if any, and the local part
    detail
        The pair by which elements and attributes are identified once prefixes have been
        resolved. `Xml.Name(t"urn:a", t"x")` is `x` in the namespace `urn:a` and
        `Xml.Name(t"x")` is an unqualified `x`; `Element#qualified`, `Xml#elements`,
        `Xml#element`, `Element#attribute` and `Xml.Reader#name` all speak in these. A name
        shows in Clark notation, `{urn:a}x`, or as the bare local part.
    entry .apply   `Xml.Name(local)`: an unqualified name, in no namespace
    entry .split   the prefix and local part of a raw `prefix:local` label, without resolving the prefix
        `(Unset, label)` for a label with no colon.
    entry .showable   Clark notation, `{uri}local`, or the bare local part
    entry .inspectable   `Name({uri}local)`
    entry #namespace   the URI, or `Unset` for a name in no namespace
    entry #local   the local part
  entry .Namespacing   whether a prefix with no binding is a parse error or resolves to no namespace
    detail
        An enumeration with the cases `Strict` and `Lenient`. The Namespaces recommendation
        requires that every prefix be bound, so the default (the companion's given) is
        `Strict`: parsing `<p:a/>` with no declaration of `p` raises a `Parse.Error` with
        `Xml.Issue.UnboundPrefix`, and an `x"…"` literal using a prefix with no `Namespace`
        given does not compile. Import `namespaceOptions.lenientNamespaces` to have an
        unbound prefix resolve to no namespace instead; `namespaceOptions.strictNamespaces`
        restates the default.
    entry .default   `Strict`
  entry .Namespaced   the namespace of a derived codec's type, as a given, for a type which cannot be annotated
    detail
        `given Rect is Xml.Namespaced = Xml.Namespaced(t"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg")` has
        `Rect`'s derived encoder write `<Rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">` with its
        fields' elements in the same namespace, and its decoder match elements by that
        resolved name. It takes precedence over an `@xmlns` annotation on the type.
        `qualified = false` leaves the fields' elements in no namespace.
    entry .apply   `Xml.Namespaced[value](uri, qualified = true)`: a namespace binding for a type
    entry .Bound   the class a `Namespaced` given is: a URI and whether fields share it
      entry #namespace   the URI
      entry #qualified   whether the fields' elements share the namespace
    entry #namespace   the URI the type's elements are in
    entry #qualified   whether the fields' elements are in the same namespace, or in none
  entry .xmlns   the annotation which puts a derived type's elements in a namespace
    detail
        `@xmlns("urn:shop") case class Order(id: Int, item: Item)` encodes as
        `<Order xmlns="urn:shop">` with its fields' elements in the same namespace, and
        decodes from any document whose elements resolve to that namespace, whatever
        prefixes it uses. `@xmlns("urn:shop", qualified = false)` on the class keeps the
        fields' elements in no namespace, as a schema with
        `elementFormDefault="unqualified"` has them (they are written with `xmlns=""`).
        The annotation is URI-keyed, never prefix-keyed. Exported as `xylophone.xmlns` and
        `soundness.xmlns`, so `@xmlns(…)` needs no qualification.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label A plain record writes unqualified elements
          code
              case class Item(sku: Text, quantity: Int)
              case class Order(id: Int, item: Item)

              Order(1, Item(t"a", 2)).in[Xml].show
              // <Order><id>1</id><item><sku>a</sku><quantity>2</quantity></item></Order>
        frame
          label Annotated, the record's elements are in the namespace
          code
              case class Item(sku: Text, quantity: Int)

              @xmlns("urn:shop")
              case class Order(id: Int, item: Item)

              Order(1, Item(t"a", 2)).in[Xml].show
              // <Order xmlns="urn:shop"><id>1</id><item><sku>a</sku><quantity>2</quantity></item></Order>
        frame
          label A document in the namespace reads back whatever its prefixes
          code
              case class Item(sku: Text, quantity: Int)

              @xmlns("urn:shop")
              case class Order(id: Int, item: Item)

              t"""<s:Order xmlns:s="urn:shop"><s:id>1</s:id><s:item><s:sku>a</s:sku><s:quantity>2</s:quantity></s:item></s:Order>"""
              . read[Order in Xml]
              // Order(1, Item(t"a", 2))
        frame
          label One field kept out of the namespace
          code
              case class Item(sku: Text, quantity: Int)

              @xmlns("urn:shop")
              case class Order(id: Int, item: Item, @unqualified reference: Text)

              Order(1, Item(t"a", 2), t"R1").in[Xml].show
              // <Order xmlns="urn:shop"><id>1</id><item>…</item><reference xmlns="">R1</reference></Order>
        ##
        ```

        A type with no namespace of its own matches its fields' elements by local name
        alone, whatever namespace the document puts them in. The direct and staged parsers
        (`Xml.Parsable.derived`, `.staged`) match raw labels and do not consult the
        annotation.
    entry #uri   the namespace URI
    entry #qualified   whether the fields' elements share the namespace (the default) or are in none
  entry .unqualified   the annotation which keeps one field's element out of its type's namespace
      `@unqualified reference: Text` on a field of an `@xmlns` type writes
      `<reference xmlns="">` and reads the element in no namespace. Exported as
      `xylophone.unqualified` and `soundness.unqualified`.
  entry .attribute   the annotation which makes a field an attribute rather than a child element
      `case class Book(title: Text, @attribute isbn: Text)` encodes as
      `<Book isbn="0441013597"><title>Dune</title></Book>` and decodes the attribute back.
      An `Optional` attribute field is simply absent when `Unset`. The annotation is reached
      as `@attribute` through the umbrella's type alias `soundness.attribute` (a term named
      `attribute` would clash with galilei's and tarantula's), as `@Xml.attribute`, or as
      `@xylophone.attribute`. The direct and staged parsers honour it too.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Book(title: Text, @attribute isbn: Text)

      Book(t"Dune", t"0441013597").in[Xml]
      // x"""<Book isbn="0441013597"><title>Dune</title></Book>"""
      ```
  entry .XmlAttribute   a typed tag's attribute: its label, the elements it applies to, or `global`
    detail
        The typeclass the typed tags (`Tag`) declare their attributes with: a
        `"href" is Xml.XmlAttribute on "a" in Schema` given says that `href` may be used on
        `<a>`, and `Element#selectDynamic` and `#updateDynamic` resolve it. Not for ordinary
        XML use; honeycomb and savagery build their vocabularies on it.
    entry #label   the attribute's name
    entry #elements   the element labels it applies to
    entry #global   whether it applies to every element
    entry #targets   whether a given tag may carry it
    entry #merge   the union of two declarations of the same attribute
    entry #Self   the attribute's label as a literal type
    entry #Topic   the attribute's value type
    entry #Plane   the element label it is declared on
  entry .decodable   the entry point for `Decodable in Xml`: a text decoder if one exists, else derivation
      An inline given which resolves, for any type, to the `Decodable in Text` bridge when
      the type has one (so `Text`, identifiers and any text-decodable type read from an
      element's text) and otherwise to the Wisteria derivation for a case class or
      enumeration. The text branch raises `Xml.Error` with `Missing` for an absent field
      and `Empty` for an element with no text, continuing with the empty text. The
      primitives have specific givens (`.int`, `.boolean`, …) which pre-empt this one, so
      that a bad number raises `Malformed` and continues with zero rather than aborting.
  entry .encodable   the entry point for `Encodable in Xml`: a text encoder if one exists, else derivation
      The mirror of `.decodable`: a type with an `Encodable in Text` (`Text`, `Int`, `Long`,
      …) becomes a leaf `Xml.Text`, and a case class or enumeration encodes through
      `EncodableDerivation`.
  entry .int   reads an `Int` from an element's text, raising `Xml.Error` and continuing with `0` on failure
      One of the primitive decoders (`.int`, `.long`, `.short`, `.byte`, `.double`,
      `.float`, `.boolean`), each capturing the `Tactic[Xml.Error]` it raises through. Text
      which is not a number raises `Malformed(text, t"Int")`; an element with no single
      text run, or a missing field, raises `Untextual(t"Int")`; each continues with a zero
      sentinel, so a record with several bad fields reports them all under
      `validate[Xml.Focus]`.
  entry .long   reads a `Long`, as `.int` reads an `Int`
  entry .short   reads a `Short`, as `.int` reads an `Int`
  entry .byte   reads a `Byte`, as `.int` reads an `Int`
  entry .double   reads a `Double`, as `.int` reads an `Int`
  entry .float   reads a `Float`, as `.int` reads an `Int`
  entry .boolean   reads a `Boolean` from the text `true` or `false`, continuing with `false` on failure
  entry .booleanEncodable   writes a `Boolean` as the text `true` or `false`
      Needed because no `Boolean is Encodable in Text` exists for the `.encodable` bridge to
      use; the other primitives encode through their text encoders.
  entry .Repeatable   marks a codec whose wire form is every same-named child, not just the first
    detail
        A mixin on a `Decodable in Xml` or `Encodable in Xml`: a repeatable field — a
        collection, a `Map`, or an `Optional` on the encoding side — is written as all the
        child elements sharing the field's name, one per item, and read from all of them in
        document order (they need not be contiguous). The product derivation recognises the
        mixin and hands a synthetic `Fragment` of the matching children to the decoder. A
        codec without the mixin reads the first matching child only.
    entry #repeatable   `true` unless overridden
  entry .collectionDecodable   reads any `Iterable` collection from every element with the field's name
      For a collection type with a stdlib `Factory` and an element type with a
      `Decodable in Xml`: each matching child decodes as one item, zero children build the
      empty collection (a missing repeated field is empty, never an error), and a lone
      element decodes as a one-item collection, which is the shape `as[List[T]]` sees at a
      document root. Repeatable.
  entry .listDecodable   reads a `List[T]`, as `.collectionDecodable` does
      The opaque Soundness collections do not conform to `Iterable`, so `List`, `Set` and
      `Sequence` each have a given of their own.
  entry .setDecodable   reads a `Set[T]`, as `.collectionDecodable` does
  entry .seriesDecodable   reads a `Sequence[T]`, as `.collectionDecodable` does
  entry .collectionEncodable   writes an `Iterable` as a `Fragment` of one node per item, for the product encoder to relabel
      Repeatable: the product encoder flattens the fragment into repeated same-named
      children. A nested collection, which has no per-item element shape, nests under an
      unnamed element which the enclosing product relabels.
  entry .listEncodable   writes a `List[T]`, as `.collectionEncodable` does
  entry .setEncodable   writes a `Set[T]`, as `.collectionEncodable` does
  entry .seriesEncodable   writes a `Sequence[T]`, as `.collectionEncodable` does
  entry .optionalDecodable   reads an `Optional[T]` field: `Unset` when the element or attribute is absent
      A missing child element (or, for an `@attribute` field, a missing attribute) decodes
      to `Unset`, or raises `Xml.Error` with `Missing` under
      `optionalityOptions.strictXmlAbsence`; a present element decodes as `T`, or as `Unset`
      when the inner decoder rejects it under `optionalityOptions.lenientXmlFaults`. XML has
      no null: an empty element is a present value, so there is no nullity option. Not
      repeatable — a present optional is the first matching child — so
      `Optional[List[T]]` is not a supported shape (a `List` field is already empty when
      absent).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      case class Tally(name: Text, count: Optional[Int])

      x"<Tally><name>Ann</name></Tally>".as[Tally]                          // Tally(t"Ann", Unset)

      locally:
        import optionalityOptions.lenientXmlFaults
        x"<Tally><name>Ann</name><count>many</count></Tally>".as[Tally]     // Tally(t"Ann", Unset)
      ```
  entry .optionalEncodable   writes an `Optional[T]` field: no element at all when `Unset`
      `Unset` encodes to an empty `Fragment`, which the repeatable-aware product encoder
      flattens to no child element (or no attribute); a present value encodes as the single
      relabelled child a mandatory field would produce.
  entry .mapEncodable   writes a `Map[K, V]` as one child per entry, each holding a `<key>` and a `<value>`
      Keys and values are any XML-encodable type, so a key need not be a valid element
      name. Each entry element is relabelled with the field's name by the product encoder.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Profile(name: Text, stock: Map[Text, Int])

      Profile(t"Ann", Map(t"apple" -> 3)).in[Xml]
      // x"<Profile><name>Ann</name><stock><key>apple</key><value>3</value></stock></Profile>"
      ```
  entry .mapDecodable   reads a `Map[K, V]` from every entry element with the field's name
      Each gathered element's `<key>` and `<value>` children decode as the key and value
      types; a missing child is handed the absent sentinel, so a primitive registers a
      focused error and an `Optional` value reads as `Unset`.
  entry .xmlDiscriminable   the default sum-type discriminator: the element's local name is the variant
      A sealed trait with cases `Book` and `Magazine` decodes from `<book …/>` and
      `<magazine …/>` by their labels, with no discriminator field; `<s:Book>` is still the
      `Book` variant, since a prefix is lexical. A label naming no variant raises
      `Xml.Error` with `UnknownVariant`. `@name[Xml](t"red")` on a variant renames its
      element. An `Xml.DiscriminantAttribute` given replaces this with an attribute.
  entry .DiscriminantAttribute   a sum discriminator which reads and writes the variant as an attribute
    detail
        `given Shape is Discriminable in Xml = Xml.DiscriminantAttribute[Shape](t"kind")`
        makes the derived codec write a `kind="Circle"` attribute on the element and read
        the variant from it, in place of the default label-as-variant scheme
        (`.xmlDiscriminable`). The staged parser recognises it too, through the staging
        compiler in `xylophone.staged`.
    entry #attribute   the attribute's name
    entry #discriminate   the attribute's value on an element, or `Unset`
    entry #rewrite   a copy of the element with the attribute set to a variant name
    entry #variant   the element itself: the variant's content is the element
  entry .DecodableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of `Decodable in Xml` for case classes and sealed types
    detail
        A product decodes from an element with a child element per field, matched by the
        field's wire name (`@name`-aware) in any order; a field marked `@attribute` reads
        from an attribute; a repeatable field gathers every matching child; a field whose
        element is missing is handed the absent sentinel, so an `Optional` reads as `Unset`,
        a primitive raises and continues, and a nested record with a `Default` substitutes it
        with a single `AbsentProduct` error rather than a cascade of sub-field errors. Text,
        comments and processing instructions between the children are ignored. A sum
        selects its variant through the `Discriminable in Xml` in scope. Each field is
        decoded under a focus naming its path, for `validate[Xml.Focus]`. Recursive types
        tie through their own derivation. Not called directly: `.decodable` reaches it.
    entry .conjunction   derives a product decoder
    entry .disjunction   derives a sum decoder
  entry .EncodableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of `Encodable in Xml`: a product is an element with a child per field
    detail
        The mirror of `.DecodableDerivation`. A product encodes to an element labelled with
        the type's short name, each field a child element labelled with the field's wire
        name (relabelling whatever its own encoder produced), an `@attribute` field an
        attribute, a repeatable field flattened to repeated children, and an `@xmlns` or
        `Xml.Namespaced` type's elements placed in its namespace with the declaration on the
        root. A sum encodes its variant and relabels the element to the variant's name, so
        it round-trips through `.xmlDiscriminable`. Not called directly: `.encodable`
        reaches it.
    entry .conjunction   derives a product encoder
    entry .disjunction   derives a sum encoder
  entry .aggregableDerived   under `import codecs.derived`, `read[T in Xml]` uses the tree path even where a direct parser exists
      One of the codec-mode givens: `codecs.derived` selects the derived tree codecs for
      every record in scope, overriding a direct instance.
  entry .aggregableDerivedText   the `Text` form of `.aggregableDerived`
  entry .readableDerived   the whole-`Data` form of `.aggregableDerived`
  entry .readableDerivedText   the whole-`Text` form of `.aggregableDerived`
  entry .encodableOverDerived   under `import codecs.derived`, `in[Text over Xml]` renders through the tree
  entry .aggregableStrict   under `import codecs.strict`, `read[T in Xml]` parses directly, or fails to compile
      Paired with `Xml1#aggregableStrictFailure`: a type with a direct codec reads through it,
      and a type with none is a compile error naming the type, rather than a silent fall
      back to the tree.
  entry .aggregableStrictText   the `Text` form of `.aggregableStrict`
  entry .encodableOverStrict   under `import codecs.strict`, `in[Text over Xml]` prints directly, or fails to compile
  entry .Parsing   the base of direct parsers: reads a value from an `Xml.Reader` with no tree built
    detail
        A distillate `Parsable` over XML: `parse(reader)` is called with the current element
        just opened on the reader (its name and attributes consumed) and must consume the
        element's content and close tag in full, through exactly one of the reader's
        content consumers (`nextChild` until `Unset`, `text`, `skipElement` or `element`).
        `Xml.Parsable` is the nominal, opt-in face (explicit instances, `Xml.Parsable.derived`,
        `derives Xml.Direct`, `codecs.direct`) and `Xml.Field` the operational one the
        product derivation resolves per field. Unlike jacinta's `Json.Parsing` there is no
        shape: XML codecs carry no morphology.
    entry #Transport   `Xml`
    entry #Reader   `Xml.Reader`
    entry #repeatable   `true` for a collection parser, gathered occurrence by occurrence
    entry #absent   what a field of this type yields when no child element or attribute carries its name
        Aborts with `Xml.Error(Missing)` unless overridden: the primitives raise and
        continue with a zero sentinel, the bridge delegates to its decoder over the absent
        sentinel, and a derived product expands per sub-field. Takes the read-site `Foci`.
    entry #attribute   how a field of this type reads from an attribute value, for an `@attribute` field
        Defaults to `absent()`, since a shape which cannot read from a text leaf treats an
        attribute as wrong-shape input, exactly as the tree path does.
  entry .Parsable   the opt-in direct parser for a type: `read[T in Xml]` reads `T` straight off the input
    detail
        A nominal `Xml.Parsing`. Once a type has an `Xml.Parsable` in scope,
        `source.read[T in Xml]` (through `Xml1#aggregableParsed`) drives it over the input
        through an `Xml.Reader` and never materialises an `Xml` tree, for the values it
        reads directly; field types with only a `Decodable in Xml` are bridged through
        `fromDecodable`, which materialises one element. There is no blanket given, so no
        read changes behaviour until a type opts in:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)
        case class Firm(name: Text, ceo: Worker)

        given Worker is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable.derived
        given Firm is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable.derived

        t"<Firm><name>Acme</name><ceo><name>Alice</name><age>30</age></ceo></Firm>".read[Firm in Xml]
        ```

        Semantics mirror the tree path exactly: wire names, `@attribute` fields,
        first-match-wins duplicates, gathering of repeated fields, defaults, absent fields
        and error foci. A case class which has both a `Reflection` and a hand-written
        `Decodable in Xml` would derive here and diverge from its decoder; the remedy is one
        line, `given MyType is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable.fromDecodable(summon[MyType is
        Decodable in Xml])`. `derived` is a method, not a given, so a type parses directly
        only once it has opted in; `derives Xml.Direct` and `import codecs.direct` are the
        other routes. The direct parsers match raw labels: `@xmlns` is not consulted.
    refer xylophone.Xml.Reader
    refer xylophone.Xml.Field
    refer xylophone.Xml.Direct
    refer xylophone.Inlinable
    entry .apply   a parser from a function over the reader
        `Xml.Parsable[T](reader => …)`: the function is called with the element opened and
        must consume it, as `Xml.Parsing#parse` must.
    entry .fromDecodable   the bridge from the tree world: materialises one element and decodes it
        The one-line escape hatch when a custom decoder must beat a derived direct parser.
        A repeatable decoder (a custom collection) keeps its gathering semantics: each
        occurrence is materialised separately and decoded together as a synthetic
        `Fragment`. Absence and attribute reads delegate to the decoder over the same
        sentinel inputs the tree derivation would hand it.
    entry .derived   a Wisteria-derived direct parser for a case class or sealed type, wrapped as nominal
        `given Foo is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable.derived`. Each field's parser resolves
        through `Xml.Field`: an explicit `Parsable`, a text codec, structural derivation, or
        the bridge over a `Decodable in Xml`, so nested records parse directly too.
    entry .staged   a macro-generated monomorphic parser for a case class, faster than `derived`
        The staged counterpart of `derived`: field values live in typed locals, child
        elements dispatch through packed-`Long` literal comparisons, and the record is built
        by a direct constructor call — no `Array[Any]` buffer, no `Mirror`, no per-field
        boxing. Same semantics as `derived`. Requires a top-level or object-nested case class
        with a single parameter list; sums, method-local classes and other shapes use
        `derived`.
    entry .fromField   wraps any `Xml.Parsing` (such as an `Xml.Field`) as a nominal `Parsable`
    entry .iterable   a direct parser for any `Iterable` collection, gathering each occurrence of the field
        Backs `.fieldCollection`; items parse through the element parser given, so nested
        products parse directly.
    entry .declared   the `Parsable` of a type which `derives Xml.Direct`
        A given resolving through the type's `Xml.Direct` instance in its companion.
    entry .Direct   the base of generated parsers, receiving the reader as a neutral carrier
      entry #parse   reads through `parseCarrier`
    entry .parseField   calls a nominal parser in a field position of a generated parser
        The call points for generated code, which is capture-erased: the parser and reader
        travel as `AnyRef` and the capability is reasserted here. Not for hand-written use.
    entry .absentField   calls a nominal parser's `absent()` from a generated parser
    entry .assemble   builds a product from an array of field values, for generated parsers
    entry .wireNames   applies `@name` renames to an array of field names, for generated parsers
    entry .missing   raises `Xml.Error(Missing)` and continues with a sentinel, for generated parsers
    entry .focusing   runs a block under a focus naming a field, for generated parsers
    entry .childIndex   the index of a child's label among a record's non-attribute field keys, or `-1`
    entry .repeats   whether a parser gathers repeated occurrences
    entry .parseElement   parses one occurrence of a repeated field, for the derived product engine
    entry .gathered   builds a repeated field's value from its gathered occurrences
    entry .product   the derived product engine: fields, a fallback default and a constructor, as an `Xml.Field`
        What `ParsableDerivation.conjunction` builds: reads attributes first, then each
        child by its wire name, gathering repeated fields, skipping unknown children and
        duplicates, and filling absent fields through their parsers or the record's
        default. Not for direct use.
  entry .Field   the per-field face of direct parsing, with a universal fallback
    detail
        The typeclass the product derivation resolves for each field. Its fallback given
        (`field`, inherited from `Xml3`) mirrors `.decodable`'s order so that a field's wire
        format is identical on the direct and tree paths: an explicit `Xml.Parsable`, a
        `Decodable in Text` codec, structural derivation, or the bridge over any remaining
        `Decodable in Xml`. `Xml.Field(parsing)` adapts any `Xml.Parsing` for use as a
        field parser.
    entry .apply   adapts an `Xml.Parsing` as a field parser, preserving its capture
  entry .intParsable   the direct `Int` parser: reads the content straight from the buffered bytes
      One of the direct primitives (`.intParsable`, `.longParsable`, `.shortParsable`,
      `.byteParsable`, `.doubleParsable`, `.floatParsable`, `.booleanParsable`), mirroring
      the tree primitives exactly: a missing or wrong-shaped element and an unparseable
      value raise `Xml.Error` (not abort) and continue with a zero sentinel, so accrual
      under `validate[Xml.Focus]` behaves identically on both paths. `Int`, `Long`,
      `Double` and `Boolean` are parsed from the bytes without materialising a `Text`.
  entry .longParsable   the direct `Long` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .shortParsable   the direct `Short` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .byteParsable   the direct `Byte` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .doubleParsable   the direct `Double` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .floatParsable   the direct `Float` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .booleanParsable   the direct `Boolean` parser, as `.intParsable`
  entry .fieldCollection   the `Xml.Field` for any `Iterable` collection: gathers every same-named child
      Declared above the fallback so a collection never reaches its `Reflection` case (a
      `List`'s own mirror would derive it as a sum). Items parse through the element's own
      field parser.
  entry .fieldList   the `Xml.Field` for a `List[T]`, as `.fieldCollection`
  entry .fieldSet   the `Xml.Field` for a `Set[T]`, as `.fieldCollection`
  entry .fieldSeries   the `Xml.Field` for a `Sequence[T]`, as `.fieldCollection`
  entry .ParsableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of `Xml.Field` for case classes and sealed types
    detail
        A product derives the direct engine (`Xml.Parsable.product`) over its fields' wire
        names, attribute flags, field parsers and defaults; a sum derives through the tree
        bridge over its `Decodable in Xml`, since variants are selected by element label.
        Reached through `Xml.Parsable.derived` and the `field` fallback.
    entry .conjunction   derives a product's direct parser
    entry .disjunction   derives a sum's parser, bridged through its decoder
  entry .Parsable2   the lower-priority layer of `Xml.Parsable`'s companion, holding `generated`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: holds the `codecs.direct` given below the companion's own
        `declared`, so a type which derives `Xml.Direct` uses the codec it generated once.
    entry #generated   under `import codecs.direct`, a parser generated for every record where one is needed
        Below `Xml.Parsable`'s own `declared`: a type which is not a record fails
        `Inlinable.Generates` and takes the derived path.
  entry .Printable2   the lower-priority layer of `Xml.Printable`'s companion, holding `generated`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use; the mirror of `Xml.Parsable2`.
    entry #generated   under `import codecs.direct`, a printer generated for every record where one is needed
  entry .Printable   the direct printer for a type: writes its compact XML text with no tree built
    detail
        The direct-printing counterpart of `Encodable in Xml`. `write(value, out)` writes the
        value as its own element and `write(value, label, out)` as a field's element,
        labelled, into an `Xml.Out`. It is reached through `in[Text over Xml]` and
        `in[Data over Xml]` (`Xml1#encodableOverPrinted`) wherever the formatting is compact;
        indented formatting still renders through the tree. Its surface is explicit
        instances, `Inlinable.printable[T]`, `derives Xml.Direct` and `import codecs.direct`.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label Through an XML tree
          code
              case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)

              Worker(t"Alice", 30).in[Text over Xml]
              // t"<Worker><name>Alice</name><age>30</age></Worker>"
        frame
          label Written directly, by a derived printer
          code
              case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)
              given Worker is Xml.Printable = Inlinable.printable[Worker]

              Worker(t"Alice", 30).in[Text over Xml]
              // the same text, written with no tree built
        frame
          label Or in blocks, as it is printed
          code
              case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)
              given Worker is Xml.Printable = Inlinable.printable[Worker]

              val blocks = scala.collection.mutable.ListBuffer[Text]()
              Xml.Printable.emit(Worker(t"Alice", 30), blocks += _)
        ##
        ```

        The printer is generated at compile time for a record with no `@name` or
        `@attribute` annotations and no namespace; other records print through the tree.
    refer xylophone.Xml.Out
    refer xylophone.Xml.Parsable
    refer xylophone.Inlinable
    entry .print   a value's compact XML text, whole, through its `Printable`
    entry .emit   a value's text in blocks of the `Buffering` capacity, each handed to a function as it fills
        No text of the whole value is built. Needs the value's `Xml.Printable` and a
        `Buffering`.
    entry .fromEncodable   the bridge from the tree world: encodes the value and writes the tree in place
        Relabels the element where it is nested, as the derivation relabels it.
    entry .declared   the `Printable` of a type which `derives Xml.Direct`
    entry .Direct   the base of generated printers, receiving the writer as a neutral carrier
      entry #write   writes through `writeCarrier`, as the value's own element or as a labelled field
    entry .writeAscii   writes ASCII markup from a generated printer
        The helpers generated printers call (`.writeAscii`, `.writeName`, `.writeLeaf`,
        `.absent`, `.nodes`, `.some`, `.writeNodes`, `.writeField`): the writer travels as an
        `AnyRef` and is reasserted here. Public because generated printers are spliced into
        user modules; not for hand-written use.
    entry .writeName   writes a name from a generated printer
    entry .writeLeaf   writes a leaf element: the label around the escaped text
    entry .absent   whether an `Optional` value is absent, and so writes no element
    entry .nodes   the nodes a field encoded through its `Encodable` contributes, relabelled and flattened
    entry .some   whether a field contributed any nodes
    entry .writeNodes   writes a field's nodes compactly
    entry .writeField   calls a nominal `Printable` in a field position of a generated printer
    entry #write   writes a value as its own element, or as a field's element with a label
  entry .Direct   the `derives` carrier for a generated parser and printer: `case class Foo(…) derives Xml.Direct`
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass has no type parameters, so it cannot appear in a `derives`
        clause itself; `Xml.Direct[Foo]` holds `Foo`'s generated `Xml.Parsable` and
        `Xml.Printable`, generated once into the companion by `derived`, and the `declared`
        givens of `Xml.Parsable` and `Xml.Printable` resolve through it with no import.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        case class Declared(id: Int, name: Text) derives Xml.Direct

        Declared(5, t"five").in[Text over Xml].read[Declared in Xml]
        ```
    entry .derived   generates the parser and printer with `Inlinable.parsable` and `Inlinable.printable`
    entry #parsable   the generated `Xml.Parsable`
    entry #printable   the generated `Xml.Printable`
  entry .Reader   the restricted rim of the parser an `Xml.Parsable` reads through
    detail
        An exclusive, stateful capability handed to `Xml.Parsing#parse` with the current
        element just opened: its name and attributes consumed. Exactly one of the content
        consumers — `nextChild` until it is `Unset`, `text` (or `int`, `long`, `double`,
        `boolean`), `skipElement` or `element` — must then consume the element's content
        and close tag in full. The reader is owned by one `parse` call at a time and nothing
        of it may be retained afterwards. It carries its own `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, so
        malformed input aborts through the read call's tactic, and the read-site
        `Tactic[Xml.Error]` and `Foci[Xml.Focus]`, so decode errors a parser raises accrue
        to the same `validate` boundary as the tree path's, with the same field foci.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

        given Point is Xml.Parsable = Xml.Parsable: reader =>
          var x = 0
          var y = 0

          def loop(): Unit = reader.nextChild() match
            case Unset => ()
            case name: Text =>
              name.s match
                case "x" => x = reader.int().or(0)
                case "y" => y = reader.int().or(0)
                case _   => reader.skipElement()
              loop()

          loop()
          Point(x, y)
        ```

        Only xylophone's read path constructs readers.
    entry .NameEnd   `-1`: the `childWord` sentinel for the close tag
    entry .NameOpaque   `-2`: the `childWord` sentinel for a name which cannot pack into a `Long`
    entry #errorTactic   the read-site `Tactic[Xml.Error]`
    entry #foci   the read-site `Foci[Xml.Focus]`
    entry #attributes   the attributes of the just-opened element, valid until the next element is opened
    entry #name   the resolved `Xml.Name` of the just-opened element, through the bindings in scope
    entry #nextChild   opens the next child element and gives its name, or `Unset` once the close tag is consumed
        Character data, comments, CDATA sections and processing instructions between child
        elements are consumed transparently.
    entry #childWord   the next child's name packed into a `Long`, or `NameEnd` or `NameOpaque`
        For staged parsers, which compare field names as literal constants; with
        `childWordHigh` for the high word, and `childLabel` for a name which cannot pack.
    entry #childWordHigh   the high word of the packed child name
    entry #childLabel   the label of the child just opened by `childWord`
    entry #text   the element's text content, consumed with its close tag, or `Unset` unless exactly one text run
        CDATA is not text here, matching the tree decoders' shape rules.
    entry #int   the content parsed as an `Int` straight from the bytes, consumed with the close tag, or `Unset`
    entry #long   the content as a `Long`, as `#int`
    entry #double   the content as a `Double`, as `#int`
    entry #boolean   the content as a `Boolean`, as `#int`
    entry #skipElement   skips the element's whole subtree, validating every close tag, building nothing
    entry #element   materialises the element as an `Xml` tree, for a type with only a `Decodable in Xml`
    entry #fault   raises an `Xml.Error` with a reason through the read-site tactic and continues

entry .Xml1   the layer of `Xml`'s companion holding the direct-parsing read path and the strict-mode failures
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `object Xml` extends it, so `Xml` inherits these givens and they
      resolve through its implicit scope; they are declared, and documented, here. It sits
      above `Xml2`, so a type with an `Xml.Parsable` or `Xml.Printable` takes the tree-free
      path wherever one exists, and below the companion's own `codecs.derived` and
      `codecs.strict` entries.
  entry #aggregableParsed   `source.read[T in Xml]` from bytes, parsed directly through `T`'s `Xml.Parsable`
      Above the tree path, so it wins whenever an `Xml.Parsable` exists:
      the value is read straight off the input through an `Xml.Reader`, with no `Xml` tree
      built. Needs the parse context, a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, a `Tactic[Xml.Error]`, a
      `Foci[Xml.Focus]` (so direct decode errors accrue with the same field foci as the
      tree path's) and a `Buffering`.
  entry #aggregableParsedText   `text.read[T in Xml]` parsed directly, as `#aggregableParsed`
  entry #readableParsed   `data.read[T in Xml]` for a whole `Data` value in hand, parsed directly
      Parses in place rather than wrapping the bytes in a stream.
  entry #readableParsedText   `text.read[T in Xml]` for a whole `Text` in hand, parsed directly
  entry #encodableOverPrinted   `value.in[Text over Xml]` written directly by the value's `Xml.Printable`
      Above `Xml2#encodableOver`: where the value has an `Xml.Printable` and
      the `Xml.Formatting` in scope is compact, the text is written with no tree built;
      under indented formatting, which generated printers do not write, it renders through
      the tree as before.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   the compile error half of `codecs.strict` for a type with no direct parser
      An inline given which, under `Codecs.Strict`, reports _xylophone_
      and the type at the use site rather than letting the derived path resolve.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailureText   the `Text` form of `#aggregableStrictFailure`
  entry #encodableOverStrictFailure   the compile error half of `codecs.strict` for a type with no direct printer

entry .Xml2   the layer of `Xml`'s companion holding the tree-materialising read path and `in[Text over Xml]`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `object Xml` extends it through `Xml1`, so `Xml` inherits these
      givens and they resolve through its implicit scope; they are declared, and documented,
      here. Below `Xml1`, so a type with no direct codec resolves here exactly as before
      direct parsing existed.
  entry #encodableOver   `value.in[Text over Xml]`: the value's XML text, through the tree
      Encodes through the `Encodable in Xml` and renders under the
      `Xml.Formatting` in scope, as `show` would. `Xml1#encodableOverPrinted` outranks it where
      the value has an `Xml.Printable` and the formatting is compact.
  entry #encodableOverData   `value.in[Data over Xml]`: the same text as bytes, in the `Codepage` in scope
  entry #aggregableIn   `source.read[T in Xml]` from bytes: parses the tree and decodes it in one step
      The shorthand for `source.read[Xml].as[T]` for any `T` with a
      `Decodable in Xml`; a stream is parsed as it arrives; a leading declaration is
      dropped. Needs the parse context, a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, a `Tactic[Xml.Error]` and a
      `Buffering`. Where `T` has an `Xml.Parsable`, `Xml1#aggregableParsed` wins and no tree is
      built.
  entry #aggregableInText   `text.read[T in Xml]`: as `#aggregableIn`, from text

entry .Xml3   the layer of `Xml`'s companion holding the universal `Xml.Field` fallback
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: declares `field`, which `Xml` inherits, at the lowest priority
      so that an explicit `Xml.Parsable`, a text codec and structural derivation all win
      over the tree bridge.
  entry #field   the universal `Xml.Field` fallback: a direct parser, a text codec, derivation, or the tree bridge
      At the lowest priority, and declared as a member of the companion's parent
      so that Wisteria's probing excludes it while deriving. A case class with both a
      `Reflection` and a custom `Decodable in Xml` derives here; give it an `Xml.Parsable`
      with `Xml.Parsable.fromDecodable` to keep its decoder.

entry .Xml4   the lowest layer of `Xml`'s companion, holding `Xml.Provider`
  detail
      Not for direct use as a trait: it declares `Provider` and `Provider2`, which
      `object Xml` inherits through `Xml1`–`Xml3`, so the provider is written
      `Xml.Provider` but is declared, and documented, here. It contributes no givens for
      `Xml` itself.
  entry #Provider2   the lower-priority layer of `Provider`'s companion: the text fallbacks for temporal and URI types
    detail
        Not for direct use: declares `Reader` and the `dateTimeText`, `dateText`,
        `timeText`, `durationText` and `anyUriText` givens, which the companion's
        interface-driven readers outrank.
    entry #Reader   the type of a builtin reader: an `Intensional.Reading` from `Xml` to a result, failing with `Xml.Provider.Error`
        `Reader[label, result]` is `Intensional.Reading[label, Xml.Provider, Xml, result,
        Xml.Provider.Error]`; each builtin type's reader is a given of it, declared in the
        provider's companion, or here for the lower-priority text fallbacks.
    entry #dateTimeText   reads `xs:dateTime` as `Text`, when no instant interface is in scope
    entry #dateText   reads `xs:date` as `Text`, when no date interface is in scope
    entry #timeText   reads `xs:time` as `Text`, when no time interface is in scope
    entry #durationText   reads `xs:duration` as `Text`, when no duration interface is in scope
    entry #anyUriText   reads `xs:anyURI` as `Text`, when no URL interface is in scope
  entry #Provider   a type provider over an XML Schema: typed records from matching documents, checked at compile time
    detail
        `object PurchaseOrder extends Xml.Provider(cp"/xsd/po.xsd")` reads the schema when
        the `record` or `tuple` macro first expands, and `PurchaseOrder.record(xml)` is a
        polyvinyl record with one typed member per child element and attribute of the
        schema's root element — `order.shipTo.city`, `order.items.item.map(_.quantity)` —
        each checked by the compiler against the schema and read lazily at the type the
        schema declares; `tuple` reads every field eagerly.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

        object PurchaseOrder extends Xml.Provider(cp"/xsd/po.xsd")

        val order = PurchaseOrder.record(document)
        order.shipTo.city                   // Text
        order.items.item.map(_.quantity)    // List[Long], a restriction of xs:positiveInteger
        order.comment                       // Optional[Text], from minOccurs="0"
        ```

        The schema parameter is an `Xml.Provider.Schema`, with conversions in its companion
        from an `Xsd`, an `Xml` document, the schema's text, or anything readable as text
        such as a classpath resource; no `XmlSchema` need be in scope where the provider is
        declared. Where a schema declares several global elements the root is the one with
        complex content, or is chosen with `root = t"Envelope"`.

        Child elements and attributes become fields by local name. A field is `Optional`
        when its element has `minOccurs="0"` or is `nillable`, and a `List` when
        `maxOccurs` allows more than one; each alternative of a `choice` is `Optional`. An
        attribute sharing a name with a child element is reached as `` `@name` ``, and the
        text of an element with simple content and attributes as `text` (`` `#text` `` if
        an attribute is called `text`). An extension's base type contributes its fields
        first. Builtin types read as `Text`, `Boolean`, `Int`, `Short`, `Byte`, `Long` (the
        integer types, with their implied bounds checked), `Double` and `Float`; the
        temporal and URI types as whatever an `Instantiable` interface in scope makes of
        them, else `Text`; `xs:list` types as `List[Text]`; and `anyType`, `xs:any`, a
        recursive type or an unresolved reference as raw `Xml`.

        A restriction's facets — `enumeration`, `pattern`, the bounds, the length facets
        and the digit facets — are checked as a field is read, raising `Xml.Provider.Error`
        with the reason, as are a missing required element and a value its type cannot
        read; every error goes through the `Tactic` of the code reading the field, so a
        `validate` or `collect` with a `venture` per field gathers every problem in a
        document. Elements are matched by resolved name: in the schema's target namespace
        where the schema qualifies the child, and in no namespace otherwise, whatever
        prefixes the document uses. Substitution groups, `xsi:type`, identity constraints
        and mixed content are read as the schema declares without them; an `import` or
        `include` is recorded in the `Xsd` but not fetched. The provider does not validate
        documents: it checks what a program reads.
    refer xylophone.Xsd
    refer xylophone.Xml.Provider.Error
    entry .string   reads `xs:string` and the token, name, identifier, QName, binary and gYear types as `Text`
    entry .boolean   reads `xs:boolean`
    entry .int   reads `xs:int` as `Int`
    entry .long   reads `xs:long`, `xs:integer` and the unsigned, positive and negative integer types as `Long`
    entry .short   reads `xs:short`
    entry .byte   reads `xs:byte`
    entry .double   reads `xs:double`
    entry .float   reads `xs:float`
    entry .decimal   reads `xs:decimal` as a `Double`
    entry .xml   reads `anyType`, `xs:any` and recursive or unresolved types as raw `Xml`
    entry .tokens   reads an `xs:list` type (`NMTOKENS`, `IDREFS`) as whitespace-separated `List[Text]`
    entry .dateTime   reads `xs:dateTime` through an `Instantiable across Instants from Text` in scope
        The temporal and URI builtins (`.dateTime`, `.date`, `.time`, `.duration`, `.anyUri`)
        read their text through the domain type's `Instantiable` found where the record is
        read (`instantInterfaces.…`, `urlInterfaces.soundnessUrl`), so a value it rejects
        fails through the `Tactic` its interface was given there; with no interface in scope
        the `…Text` readers give the text.
    entry .date   reads `xs:date` through an `Instantiable across Dates from Text`
    entry .time   reads `xs:time` through an `Instantiable across Times from Text`
    entry .duration   reads `xs:duration` through an `Instantiable across Durations from Text`
    entry .anyUri   reads `xs:anyURI` through an `Instantiable across Urls from Text`
    entry .RestrictedText   the reader of a restricted string type, checking its length, pattern and enumeration facets
      entry #transform   reads the text and checks the facets, raising `Xml.Provider.Error`
    entry .RestrictedInt   the reader of a restricted `xs:int`, checking its bounds, digits and enumeration
      entry #transform   reads the value and checks the facets
    entry .RestrictedLong   the reader of a restricted integer type, checking its bounds, digits and enumeration
      entry #transform   reads the value and checks the facets
    entry .RestrictedDouble   the reader of a restricted decimal or floating type, checking its facets
      entry #transform   reads the value and checks the facets
    entry .restrictedText   the given `RestrictedText`
    entry .restrictedInt   the given `RestrictedInt`
    entry .restrictedLong   the given `RestrictedLong`
    entry .restrictedDouble   the given `RestrictedDouble`
    entry .Layout   the fields of a schema's root, with the namespaces its children and attributes are expected in
      entry #fields   the root's fields as polyvinyl members, by name
      entry #elements   the namespace expected of each child element, keyed by parent and child local name
      entry #attributes   the namespace expected of each attribute, keyed by parent and attribute local name
    entry .layout   walks an `Xsd` to the `Layout` of its root element
    entry .fieldsOf   the fields of a schema's root, as `layout(xsd, root).fields`
    entry .Schema   the schema a provider is built from: an `Xsd`, an `Xml` document, text, or any text source
      detail
          The `into` parameter type of `Xml.Provider`: the companion's conversions apply at
          the call, so `Xml.Provider(cp"/xsd/po.xsd")`, `Xml.Provider(xsd)`,
          `Xml.Provider(document)` and `Xml.Provider(text)` all work. A source is read as text
          and parsed with the freeform vocabulary when the provider first reads its schema;
          a schema which cannot be parsed is reported as a `Specification.Error` — a compile
          error at the `record` use site.
      entry .xsd   a `Schema` from a parsed `Xsd`
      entry .xml   a `Schema` from an `Xml` document of a schema
      entry .text   a `Schema` from the schema's text
      entry #xsd   the parsed schema, raising `Specification.Error` if it cannot be read
    entry .Schema2   the lower-priority layer of `Schema`'s companion, holding the `Readable` conversion
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use.
      entry #readable   a `Schema` from anything `Readable to Text`, such as a classpath resource
          At lower priority than the three direct conversions in `Schema`'s companion.
    entry .Error   raised when a record's field does not conform to its schema
      detail
          Error 993: _the XML did not conform to its schema because `reason`_. Raised through
          the `Tactic[Xml.Provider.Error]` of the code reading a record field — so
          `attempt[Xml.Provider.Error](record.shipTo.city)` catches one, and a `collect` with
          a `venture` per field gathers every violation — with a reason from
          `Xml.Provider.Error.Reason`.
      entry .Reason   why a field did not conform, each with its number
          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `OutOfRange(value, minimum, maximum)`  1  _the value `value` is outside the range `minimum` to `maximum`_  a bound facet, or an integer type's implied bound
          row  `LengthOutOfRange(value, minimum, maximum)`  2  _the length of `value` is outside the range `minimum` to `maximum`_
          row  `PatternMismatch(value, pattern)`  3  _the value `value` does not match the pattern `pattern`_
          row  `NotPermitted(value, permitted)`  4  _the value `value` is not one of `permitted`_  an enumeration facet
          row  `TooManyDigits(value, total, fraction)`  5  _the value `value` has more digits than the `total` total or `fraction` fraction digits permitted_
          row  `Absent(name)`  6  _the element or attribute `name` is required_  a required element or attribute
          row  `Malformed(value, expected)`  7  _the text `value` is not a valid `expected`_, or _the value has no text to read as `expected`_  a value its type cannot read; `value` is `Unset` for an element with no text
          ```

          An unset bound renders as _…_.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   the `Xml.Provider.Error.Reason`
    entry #Origin   `Xml`: what a record is read from
    entry #Form   `Xml.Provider`
    entry #Failure   `Xml.Provider.Error`: what a field raises
    entry #xsd   the parsed schema, raising `Specification.Error` if it cannot be read
    entry #fields   the root element's fields as polyvinyl members, for the `record` macro
    entry #access   the `Xml` a field reads: a child by resolved name, an attribute, or own text
        `access(name, xml)` selects the first child the name resolves to in the namespace
        the schema expects, an attribute for `` `@name` `` (or for a name with no such
        child), or the element's own text for `` `#text` `` and `text`; a `nil` child or a
        missing one gives the absent sentinel.
    entry #absent   whether a value is the absent sentinel
    entry #required   the value, or `Xml.Provider.Error` with `Absent(name)` if it is absent
    entry #repeated   every non-nil child the name resolves to, for a `List` field

entry .x   the `x"…"` interpolator: an XML literal parsed and checked as the code compiles, and a pattern
  detail
      `x"<user>$name</user>"` parses the literal while the code compiles, so a malformed
      fragment is a compile error at the exact span at fault, and substitutes each hole by
      the context it lands in: a `Text` or any `Encodable in Xml` value as content, an
      `Xml` as a child node, a `Text` as an attribute value. A value which cannot be
      inserted where it stands is rejected with `Xml.Issue.BadInsertion`. The result is an
      `Xml` (a `Fragment` of the literal's nodes). A prefix the literal uses but does not
      declare takes its binding from the `Namespace` givens in scope, and an unbound prefix
      does not compile. Needs no schema or scope in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val name = t"Alice"
      x"<user>$name</user>"
      x"<?stylesheet href='a.xsl'?><root/>"
      ```

      As a pattern, `x"…"` matches an `Xml` value against the literal's shape and binds
      its holes: `$text` in content binds the node (`Xml.Text(t"hello")` or a child
      element), `<$element>` binds a whole child element, `id=$value` binds an attribute's
      value as `Text`, `<!--$text-->` and `<![CDATA[$text]]>` bind their content, and a
      bare `$attrs` inside a start tag binds the remaining attributes as a
      `Map[Text, Text]`. A literal with no holes matches as a `Boolean`; a tag, child count
      or literal attribute value which differs falls through to the next case.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val scrutinee: Xml = x"""<a id="5"><b>1</b></a>"""

      scrutinee match
        case x"<a id=$value>$child</a>" => (value, child)   // (t"5", x"<b>1</b>")
        case _                          => (t"", x"<none/>")
      ```

      A hole inside CDATA or comment text, in a processing instruction, or a `DOCTYPE`
      pattern is rejected.
  refer xylophone.Xml.interpolator
  refer xylophone.Xml.extrapolator
  refer xylophone.Namespace

entry .xp   the `xp"…"` interpolator: an XPath expression checked as the code compiles
    `xp"/root[1]/child[2]"` is an `XPath` parsed while the code compiles, so a malformed
    expression is a compile error. A step's ordinal defaults to the first match, `@` names
    an attribute, and the full XPath 1.0 grammar — axes, predicates on attributes and text,
    the core function library — is accepted. A prefixed name test (`xp"//svg:rect"`) takes
    its binding from the `Namespace` givens in scope and matches by namespace;
    `XPath#in(scope)` rebinds a path's prefixes. The result is evaluated against a document
    with `Xml.select`, `Xml.selectText` and `Xml.evaluate`, and is what positions and
    accrued errors are reported against.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    xp"/root[1]/child[2]".encode   // t"/root[1]/child[2]"
    xp"/root/child".encode         // t"/root[1]/child[1]"
    ```

entry .attribute   the `@attribute` annotation, as `xylophone.attribute`; see `Xml.attribute`
    Exported from `Xml` so a field is annotated `@attribute` with no qualification; the
    umbrella exports it as the type alias `soundness.attribute`.

entry .xmlns   the `@xmlns` annotation, as `xylophone.xmlns`; see `Xml.xmlns`
    Exported from `Xml`, and from the umbrella as `soundness.xmlns`.

entry .unqualified   the `@unqualified` annotation, as `xylophone.unqualified`; see `Xml.unqualified`
    Exported from `Xml`, and from the umbrella as `soundness.unqualified`.

entry .construables   the `Construable` givens which parse an HTTP body with an XML media type as `Xml`
  detail
      In `xylophone.http`. A telekinesis response or request body whose media type is
      `application/xml` or `text/xml` constructs as an `Xml` value through these, with the
      parse context in scope. Imported by name: `import construables.xmlConstruable`.
  entry .xmlConstruable   `application/xml` bodies construct as `Xml`
  entry .textXmlConstruable   `text/xml` bodies construct as `Xml`

entry .postables   the `Postable` given which sends an `Xml` value as an HTTP request body
  detail
      In `xylophone.http`. Imported by name: `import postables.xmlPostable`.
  entry .xmlPostable   an `Xml` value as a request body: `application/xml; charset=UTF-8`, its `show` encoded in the `Codepage` in scope

entry .servables   the `Servable` given which serves an `Xml` value as an HTTP response body
  detail
      In `xylophone.http`. Imported by name: `import servables.xmlServable`.
  entry .xmlServable   an `Xml` value as a fixed response body: `application/xml; charset=UTF-8`, encoded in the `Codepage` in scope

entry .InlinableStager   the in-macro staging compiler which evaluates an `Inlinable` instance at expansion time
  hidden
  detail
      In `xylophone.staged`; not for direct use. `Inlinable.Stager` loads
      `InlinableStager.Implementation` by name when the component is on the macro
      classpath, so that a generated direct parser (`Inlinable.parsable`,
      `Xml.Parsable.staged`, `codecs.direct`) can call a user-written `Inlinable` given's
      `parse` while expanding, and read a sum's `Xml.DiscriminantAttribute`. Each summon
      runs a fresh compiler over the macro classloader with the current output directory
      excluded, so a type defined in the compilation run in progress is refused and the
      generator degrades to a runtime seam. A successful summon is reported as a compiler
      info message with its duration.
  entry .evaluate   summons the `Inlinable` for a field type in a staged compiler, or `None`
  entry .discriminantAttribute   the discriminator attribute of a sum whose `Discriminable in Xml` is an `Xml.DiscriminantAttribute`
  entry .Implementation   the `Inlinable.Stager` loaded by name
    entry .summon   `InlinableStager.evaluate`
    entry .discriminantAttribute   `InlinableStager.discriminantAttribute`

entry .XPath   an XPath 1.0 expression as a value: parsed, built with typed constructors, evaluated against an `Xml`
  detail
      `XPath` is the whole of the XPath 1.0 expression language as an immutable value: a
      location path (`/app/div[1]/@id`), a filter path (`(//a)[1]/b`), or any expression over
      the four value types — node-set, boolean, number and string — such as `count(//li)` or
      `//li = 'two'`. It is a `Format`, so the text form parses with `as[XPath]` (raising a
      `Parse.Error` with the character offset of the fault) and renders with `encode`, and the
      `xp"…"` interpolator checks the expression as the code compiles. An `Xml` answers a path
      through `select` (the matching nodes, as a `Fragment` in document order), `selectText`
      (the string-value of the first match) and `evaluate` (an `XPath.Value` of any kind).

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Every list item in the document
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

            val page: Xml =
              t"""<app><div id="main"><button data-test="submit">Submit</button>
                  <ul><li n="1">one</li><li n="2">two</li><li n="3">three</li></ul></div></app>"""
              . read[Xml]

            page.evaluate(xp"count(//li)")   // XPath.Value.Numeric(3)
      frame
        label Narrowed by an attribute predicate
        code
            page.evaluate(xp"count(//li[@n > 1])")   // XPath.Value.Numeric(2)
      frame
        label The last of those, by position
        code
            page.selectText(xp"//li[@n > 1][position() = last()]")   // t"three"
      frame
        label Stepping up to the enclosing element's attribute
        code
            page.selectText(xp"//li[@n > 1][position() = last()]/../../@id")   // t"main"
      ##
      ```

      An expression is a tree of `XPath.Expression` nodes; a path is an `Expression.Route` of
      `XPath.Step`s from an `XPath.Origin`, each step an `XPath.Axis`, an `XPath.NodeTest` and
      its predicates. The abbreviations `//`, `.`, `..` and `@` are desugared on parsing and
      restored on rendering, so `xp"//a/@href".encode` is `//a/@href`. Where a path is
      assembled rather than written, the companion's constructors read close to XPath
      itself: `XPath / t"html" / t"body"` is `/html/body`, `XPath.deep(t"div")` is `//div`,
      and `#where` adds a predicate built from `XPath.attribute`, `XPath.textual` and the
      operators on `Expression`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val locator: XPath =
        XPath.deep(t"div").where(XPath.attribute(t"class").contains(t"button")
          and XPath.textual === t"Submit")

      locator.encode   // t"//div[contains(@class,'button') and text()='Submit']"
      ```

      All thirteen axes but `namespace` are evaluated, as are the core function library
      (`count`, `name`, `local-name`, `namespace-uri`, `string`, `concat`, `starts-with`,
      `contains`, `substring-before`, `substring-after`, `substring`, `string-length`,
      `normalize-space`, `translate`, `boolean`, `not`, `true`, `false`, `lang`, `number`,
      `sum`, `floor`, `ceiling`, `round`, `position` and `last`) and variables, which
      `evaluate` takes as a `Map` of `XPath.Value`s. The `id()` function and the `namespace`
      axis raise `XPath.Error` as unsupported, as does a variable with no binding.

      A path carries a `#scope`, the `Xml.Scope` which binds the prefixes its name tests use:
      a prefixed test whose prefix the scope binds matches by resolved namespace and local
      name, whatever prefix the document uses, and one whose prefix is unbound matches the raw
      label. An `xp"…"` literal binds its prefixes through the `Namespace` givens at its call
      site, and `#in` rebinds them.

      Malformed *text* is a `Parse.Error` over this format, with an `XPath.Issue` saying what
      was wrong; a failure to *evaluate* an expression which parsed is an `XPath.Error`.
  refer xylophone.Xml
  refer xylophone.XPath.Value
  refer xylophone.XPath.Expression
  refer xylophone.Namespace
  refer xylophone.xp
  entry .name   the format's name, `XPath`, as `Parse.Error` messages name it
  entry .focusable   lets an `XPath` stand as a `Focusable` locator, with the strategy `xpath`
      The given by which a browser-automation library takes an `XPath` as a locator: its
      strategy is `xpath` and its focus text is the rendered expression.
  entry .Position   a point in an XPath's text, as a `Parse.Error` reports it
    detail
        A `Format.Position` whose `Span` is in offset mode — a character index into the
        expression, since an XPath has no lines — and zero-length, since the parser detects a
        fault at a point rather than over a range. The `xp"…"` interpolator maps it back onto a
        caret in the source file.
    entry .at   the position at a character offset, as a zero-length span
    entry #span   the span, in offset mode and zero-length
    entry #describe   the position as text: `character 7`, or `an unknown position`
  entry .Axis   the thirteen XPath 1.0 axes, each with its keyword
    detail
        `Ancestor`, `AncestorOrSelf`, `Attribute`, `Child`, `Descendant`, `DescendantOrSelf`,
        `Following`, `FollowingSibling`, `Namespace`, `Parent`, `Preceding`,
        `PrecedingSibling` and `Self`, whose `keyword` is the spelling in the unabbreviated
        `axis::test` syntax (`following-sibling`). Every axis but `Namespace` is evaluated;
        a step on the `Namespace` axis raises `XPath.Error` with `Reason.Unsupported`.
    entry #keyword   the axis's spelling in `axis::test` syntax, such as `descendant-or-self`
  entry .NodeTest   what a step matches: a name, a wildcard, or a node kind
      ```tabular
      column  Node test
      column  XPath
      column  Matches
      row  `Name(prefix, local)`  `local` or `prefix:local`  an element, or on the attribute axis an attribute, by name
      row  `Wildcard`  `*`  any element, or any attribute on the attribute axis
      row  `PrefixWildcard(prefix)`  `prefix:*`  any element or attribute in the prefix's namespace
      row  `Node`  `node()`  any node
      row  `Textual`  `text()`  a text node; named so as not to shadow `Text`
      row  `Comment`  `comment()`  a comment
      row  `Instruction(target)`  `processing-instruction('target')`, or `processing-instruction()` when the target is `Unset`  a processing instruction
      ```

      A name test's principal node type follows the axis: attributes on the attribute axis,
      elements on every other.
  entry .Step   one location step: an axis, a node test and its predicates
    detail
        Steps are held root-first, in source order, with the abbreviations desugared: `//`
        is a `descendant-or-self::node()` step, `.` is `self::node()`, `..` is
        `parent::node()` and `@name` is `attribute::name`. A bare `Text` converts to a
        `child::name` step through `XPath.stepConversion`, so `XPath / t"html"` needs no
        `Step` written out.
    entry #axis   the step's axis
    entry #test   the step's node test
    entry #predicates   the step's predicates, applied in order; `Nil` for none
  entry .Origin   where a path starts: the document root, the context node, or a filter expression
      `Root` is an absolute path's `/`, the virtual node above the document element; `Here`
      is the context node a relative path starts from; and `Filter(expression, predicates)`
      is the head of a filter path such as `(//a)[1]/b`, whose predicates apply to the
      expression's node-set before the steps.
  entry .Expression   the XPath 1.0 expression tree, with operators for building one in code
    detail
        Every production of the expression grammar is a case: the boolean operators `Or`
        and `And`; the comparisons `Equal`, `Unequal`, `Less`, `LessOrEqual`, `Greater` and
        `GreaterOrEqual`; the arithmetic `Add`, `Subtract`, `Multiply`, `Divide`, `Modulo`
        and `Negate`; `Union` (`|`); the primaries `Literal(text)`, `Number(value)`,
        `Variable(prefix, name)` and `Call(prefix, name, arguments)`; `Route(origin, steps)`,
        which is any location or filter path; and `Substitution(index)`, which marks an
        `xp"…"` insertion during macro expansion and never appears in a decoded or evaluated
        expression.

        The operator methods build the same tree from code, taking anything which converts
        to an `Expression`: a `Text` becomes a string literal, an `Int` or `Double` a number,
        and an `XPath` its expression, so a predicate reads much as it would in XPath.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val predicate: XPath.Expression =
          XPath.attribute(t"n") > 1 and XPath.position === XPath.last

        XPath.deep(t"li").where(predicate).encode   // t"//li[@n>1 and position()=last()]"
        ```

        They are members rather than extensions because `import soundness.*` brings in
        generic extensions of the same names (`contains`, `===`, `<`), which an extension
        here could not outrank.
    entry #or   `left or right`
    entry #and   `left and right`
    entry #===   `left = right`, XPath equality, with node-set comparison existential
    entry #!==   `left != right`
    entry #<   `left < right`, compared as numbers
    entry #<=   `left <= right`
    entry #>   `left > right`
    entry #>=   `left >= right`
    entry #+   `left + right`
    entry #-   `left - right`
    entry #*   `left * right`
    entry #|   `left | right`, the union of two node-sets
    entry #contains   the call `contains(left, right)`
    entry #startsWith   the call `starts-with(left, right)`
  entry .Location   the simple positional view of a path: named elements by rank, and one attribute
      `Element(name, rank)` and `Attribute(name)`: the subset of a path — `child::name` steps
      with integer ordinals, optionally ending in `@name` — which positions recorded by
      `parsing.trackPositions` can be resolved against, and which the derived decoders
      report an error's location as.
  entry .stepConversion   a `Text` as a `child::name` step
      The `Conversion` which lets `XPath / t"html"` and `path.deep(t"a")` take a name
      directly: `t"div"` is `Step(Axis.Child, NodeTest.Name(Unset, t"div"))`.
  entry .literalConversion   a `Text` as a string literal in an expression
      So that `XPath.textual === t"Submit"` is `text()='Submit'`. Which conversion applies
      to a `Text` is decided by the expected type: a `Step` where a path takes a step, an
      `Expression` where a predicate takes one.
  entry .integerConversion   an `Int` as a number in an expression
  entry .decimalConversion   a `Double` as a number in an expression
  entry .pathConversion   an `XPath` as the expression it holds
      So a path can stand in a predicate: `XPath.deep(t"li").where(XPath.relative(t"b"))`
      is `//li[b]`.
  entry ./   `XPath / step`: an absolute path, beginning at the root
      `XPath / t"html" / t"body"` is `/html/body`: the companion's `/` starts the path at
      `Origin.Root`, and each further `/` on the result appends a `child::` step.
  entry .deep   `XPath.deep(step)`: a path beginning `//`
      `XPath.deep(t"div")` is `//div`: a `descendant-or-self::node()` step from the root,
      then the step.
  entry .relative   `XPath.relative(step)`: a path from the context node
      `XPath.relative(t"div")` is `div`, a relative path, as a predicate or a step from
      wherever it is evaluated.
  entry .here   the context node, `.`
      Usable as a path, or through `pathConversion` in a predicate.
  entry .attribute   the predicate `@name`: an attribute of the context node
      `XPath.attribute(t"class")` is `@class`, for predicates such as
      `XPath.attribute(t"class").contains(t"active")`; the instance method `#attribute`
      is the path step `/@name` instead.
  entry .textual   the predicate `text()`: the context node's text children
      `XPath.textual === t"Submit"` is `text()='Submit'`.
  entry .position   the call `position()`
  entry .last   the call `last()`
  entry .function   a call of any function by name, `XPath.function(t"count")(…)`
      The arguments take anything which converts to an `Expression`. An unknown function
      name parses and renders, and raises `XPath.Error` with `Reason.UnknownFunction` when
      evaluated.
  entry .variable   a variable reference, `$name`
      Bound at evaluation by the `variables` map `Xml.evaluate` takes; an unbound variable
      raises `XPath.Error` with `Reason.UnboundVariable`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      val doc = t"<r/>".read[Xml]
      doc.evaluate(XPath(XPath.variable(t"n") + 1), Map(t"n" -> XPath.Value.Numeric(2)))
      // XPath.Value.Numeric(3)
      ```
  entry .Locus   a node found by evaluation, with its place in the document
    detail
        The members of a node-set: the evaluation subject (`#subject`, `Unset` for the
        virtual root above the document element), the `#document` it was found in, and
        `#path`, the child-index path from the virtual root down to the node. The index path
        supplies what the node tree itself cannot — parentage, document order and identity
        — since nodes carry no parent pointers and a node equals a singleton `Fragment` of
        it. An attribute is a pseudo-node which shares its owner element's path, holds the
        owner as its subject, and sets `#attributeIndex`.
    entry #document   the document the node was found in
    entry #path   the child indices from the virtual root down to the node
    entry #subject   the node itself, or `Unset` for the virtual root
    entry #attributeIndex   the index of the attribute within its element, for an attribute locus
    entry #stringValue   the XPath string-value: an element's descendant text, an attribute's value
        For an element, every descendant text and CDATA node concatenated; for a text,
        CDATA, comment or processing-instruction node, its own content; for an attribute,
        its value; and for the virtual root, the string-value of the whole document.
  entry .Value   the result of evaluating an expression: a node-set, boolean, number or string
    detail
        The four XPath 1.0 value types: `NodeSet(loci)`, always in document order without
        duplicates; `Truth(value)`; `Numeric(value)`, a `Double`; and `Textual(value)`. The
        conversions of the specification are members, so a value of any kind can be read as
        any other, as `boolean()`, `number()` and `string()` would.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        val doc = t"<r><x>1</x><x>2</x></r>".read[Xml]
        doc.evaluate(xp"count(//x)") == XPath.Value.Numeric(2)
        doc.evaluate(xp"//x").text      // t"1", the first node's string-value
        doc.evaluate(xp"//x").truth     // true: the node-set is not empty
        ```
    entry #truth   the value as a boolean: a non-empty node-set, a non-zero number, a non-empty string
    entry #number   the value as a number: `true` is 1, a string parses as XPath `number()`, else NaN
    entry #text   the value as a string: a node-set's first string-value, `1` for `1.0`, `true`/`false`
  entry .Error   raised when an expression which parsed cannot be evaluated
    detail
        Error 563: _the XPath could not be evaluated because `reason`_. Raised by `Xml.select`,
        `Xml.selectText` and `Xml.evaluate`, each of which takes a `Tactic[XPath.Error]`, for
        an unknown function, a wrong number of arguments, an unbound variable, a
        non-node-set given to `select`, an unsupported feature (the `id()` function, the
        `namespace` axis) or an expression still holding an unresolved substitution.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        val doc = unsafely(t"<r/>".read[Xml])
        safely(doc.evaluate(xp"frobnicate()"))   // Unset: an unknown function
        ```
    entry .Reason   why evaluation failed, with the number which distinguishes it
        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `UnknownFunction(name)`  1  _the function `name` is not an XPath 1.0 core function_
        row  `BadArity(name)`  2  _the function `name` was applied to the wrong number of arguments_
        row  `UnboundVariable(name)`  3  _the variable $`name` has no binding_
        row  `NotNodeSet`  4  _a node-set was expected_  raised by `select` when the expression's value is not a node-set
        row  `Unsupported(feature)`  5  _`feature` is not supported_
        row  `Unresolved`  6  _the expression contains an unresolved substitution_
        ```
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   the `XPath.Error.Reason` which stopped evaluation
  entry .interpolable   the given behind `xp"…"`: parses and checks the literal as the code compiles
      Parses the literal at expansion time with the same parser the runtime `Decodable`
      uses, so the compiletime check cannot diverge from runtime behavior, and maps a
      failure's offset back to the offending character in the source. Substitutions are
      permitted wherever the grammar allows a primary expression, and the parsed tree, with
      the insertions grafted in, is lifted into the code, so nothing is re-parsed at
      runtime. Prefixes the literal uses are bound through the `Namespace` givens in scope.
  entry .decodable   parses any XPath 1.0 expression from text, as `as[XPath]`
      Needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`: a malformed expression raises a `Parse.Error` over the
      `XPath` format, whose `XPath.Issue` says what was wrong and whose `XPath.Position`
      gives the character offset.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"//li[position()=last()]".as[XPath].encode
      ```
  entry .Issue   what was wrong with an XPath's text, in a `Parse.Error`
    detail
        `UnexpectedCharacter`, `UnterminatedLiteral`, `UnknownAxis` (not one of the thirteen
        axes), `ExpectedNodeTest`, `ExpectedExpression`, `ExpectedCloseParen`,
        `ExpectedCloseBracket`, `UnexpectedEnd` and `UnexpectedToken`. Each is a
        `Format.Issue` with a `describe` message; the position is the `XPath.Position` at
        which the parser detected it.
    entry #describe   the issue as a message, such as _the string literal is not terminated_
  entry #expression   the expression tree; an empty absolute path, `/`, when none is given
  entry #scope   the `Xml.Scope` binding the prefixes the path's name tests use
      Defaults to `Xml.Scope.xml`, which binds only the reserved `xml` prefix. A prefixed name
      test matches by resolved name when its prefix is bound here, and by raw label otherwise.
  entry #in   the same path with its prefixes rebound to another scope
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      val doc = t"""<r xmlns:a="urn:a"><a:x>1</a:x><x>3</x></r>""".read[Xml]
      doc.select(xp"//z:x".in(Xml.Scope(t"z" -> t"urn:a")))   // the one <a:x>
      ```
  entry #/   `path / step`: appends a `child::` step
      `XPath / t"html" / t"body"` is `/html/body`. On an expression which is not a path, the
      expression becomes the head of a filter path, as `(//a)[1]/b` has it.
  entry #deep   `path.deep(step)`: appends `//` and a step
      `XPath.deep(t"div").deep(t"a")` is `//div//a`.
  entry #on   appends a step on any axis with any node test
      `path.on(XPath.Axis.FollowingSibling, XPath.NodeTest.Name(Unset, t"li"))` appends
      `following-sibling::li`, for the axes the abbreviated forms do not reach.
  entry #where   adds a predicate to the final step
      `XPath.deep(t"div").where(XPath.attribute(t"id") === t"main")` is `//div[@id='main']`.
      The predicate is anything which converts to an `Expression`: a `Text` is a string
      literal, an `Int` a number, and an `XPath` a relative path. A path with no steps takes
      the predicate on its filter expression instead.
  entry #apply   `path(n)`: a positional predicate
      `(XPath / t"li")(2)` is `/li[2]`.
  entry #element   appends `name[ordinal]`, the ordinal defaulting to 1
      The step the derived decoders build an error's location from: `path.element(t"child")`
      is `path/child[1]`.
  entry #attribute   appends `@name`, selecting an attribute
      `(XPath / t"root").attribute(t"id")` is `/root/@id`. A path ending in an attribute
      step is read with `Xml.selectText` or `Xml.evaluate`, since attributes are not tree
      nodes for `Xml.select` to return.

entry .Xsd   an XML Schema (XSD 1.0) as an immutable model of its declarations
  detail
      `Xsd.parse` reads a schema document into a value: its global `#elements`,
      `#complexTypes`, `#simpleTypes`, `#attributes`, `#groups` and `#attributeGroups`, each a
      `Map` by name in declaration order, with the `#targetNamespace`, the two form defaults,
      the `#imports` and `#includes` it names, and its top-level `#documentation`. References
      — a `type="tns:Order"`, a `ref="tns:item"`, a `base` — are kept as resolved
      `Xml.Name`s and looked up on demand with `#resolve`, so a recursive schema is
      representable and the parse is a single pass; imports and includes are recorded, not
      fetched. Elements are dispatched by resolved name, so `xs:`, `xsd:` and a default XSD
      namespace all read alike.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      val schema: Xsd = Xsd.parse(cp"/xsd/po.xsd".read[Text])
      Map.keys(schema.elements)                      // Set(t"purchaseOrder", t"comment")
      schema.element(t"purchaseOrder").let(_.typeRef)
      schema.resolve(Xml.Name(Xsd.namespace, t"int"))   // Xsd.SimpleType.Builtin(Xsd.Builtin.Int)
      ```

      The model is what an `Xml.Provider` reads its typed records' fields from; it does not
      validate documents. A schema document which is not a schema, or which breaks the
      grammar the reader follows, raises `Xsd.Error`.
  refer xylophone.Xml
  refer xylophone.XmlSchema
  entry .namespace   the XML Schema namespace, `http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema`
  entry .instance   the instance namespace, `http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance`
  entry .Form   whether local elements or attributes are namespace-qualified: `Qualified` or `Unqualified`
  entry .Use   how an attribute may appear: `Optional`, `Required` or `Prohibited`
  entry .Occurs   how many times a particle may occur: `minOccurs` and `maxOccurs`
    detail
        `max` is `Unset` for `unbounded`; both default to 1, as the schema's attributes do.
    entry #min   the minimum, from `minOccurs`
    entry #max   the maximum, from `maxOccurs`; `Unset` for `unbounded`
    entry #optional   whether the minimum is zero
    entry #many   whether more than one occurrence is allowed
  entry .Builtin   the builtin datatypes of XML Schema Part 2, by local name
    detail
        One case per builtin, from `String`, `Boolean`, `Decimal`, `Float` and `Double`
        through the date, time and duration types, the binary and URI types, the token, name
        and identifier types, the integer hierarchy (`Integer`, `Long`, `Int`, `Short`,
        `Byte`, the non-positive, negative, non-negative, positive and unsigned forms) to
        `AnyType` and `AnySimpleType`. `Xsd.Builtin(t"positiveInteger")` finds one by its local
        name, and `Xsd#builtin` by a resolved name in the XSD namespace.
    entry .apply   the builtin with a local name, or `Unset`
    entry #local   the type's local name in the XSD namespace, such as `nonNegativeInteger`
  entry .TypeRef   how a declaration names its type: by name, or by an inline definition
      `Named(name)` is a `type="…"` attribute, resolved to an `Xml.Name`; `Inline(definition)`
      is a `complexType` or `simpleType` written inside the declaration.
  entry .Type   a complex or simple type: the sealed parent of `ComplexType` and `SimpleType`
  entry .SimpleType   a simple type: a builtin, a restriction, a list, or a union
      `Builtin(builtin)`; `Restriction(base, facets)`; `Sequence(item)`, an `xs:list` of an
      item type; and `Union(members)`.
  entry .SimpleDecl   a named `simpleType` declaration
    entry #name   the type's name, or `Unset` for an anonymous one
    entry #definition   the `SimpleType` it declares
    entry #documentation   the text of its `annotation/documentation` children
  entry .Facet   a constraining facet of a restriction
      `Enumeration(values)`, `Pattern(regex)`, `MinInclusive`, `MaxInclusive`,
      `MinExclusive` and `MaxExclusive` (each holding the value as text), `MinLength`,
      `MaxLength` and `Length`, `TotalDigits` and `FractionDigits`, and `WhiteSpace(mode)`.
      An `Xml.Provider` checks each as a field is read.
  entry .Particle   a content-model term: a compositor, an element, a group reference or a wildcard
    detail
        `Sequence(items, occurs)`, `Choice(items, occurs)` and `All(items, occurs)` are the
        compositors; `Element(declaration, occurs)` a local or referenced element;
        `Group(ref, occurs)` a reference to a named group; and `Any(occurs)` an `xs:any`
        wildcard.
    entry #occurs   how many times the particle may occur
  entry .ElementDecl   an element declaration, global or local
    detail
        A global element has a `#name`; a local one may instead be a `#ref` to a global one.
        Its type is a `#typeRef`, by name or inline, or `Unset` for `anyType`.
    entry #name   the element's name, or `Unset` for a reference
    entry #ref   the global element this one refers to, or `Unset`
    entry #typeRef   the element's type, by name or inline, or `Unset`
    entry #nillable   whether `xsi:nil` is permitted
    entry #default   the `default` value, as text
    entry #fixed   the `fixed` value, as text
    entry #form   the element's own `form`, overriding the schema's default, or `Unset`
    entry #substitutionGroup   the head of the substitution group the element belongs to
    entry #abstractDecl   whether the element is declared `abstract`
    entry #documentation   the text of its `annotation/documentation` children
  entry .AttributeDecl   an attribute declaration, global or local
    entry #name   the attribute's name, or `Unset` for a reference
    entry #ref   the global attribute this one refers to, or `Unset`
    entry #typeRef   the attribute's type, by name or inline, or `Unset`
    entry #use   `Optional`, `Required` or `Prohibited`
    entry #default   the `default` value, as text
    entry #fixed   the `fixed` value, as text
    entry #form   the attribute's own `form`, or `Unset`
  entry .AttributeUse   an attribute use in a complex type: a declaration, a group reference, or `anyAttribute`
      `Attribute(declaration)`, `Group(ref)` and `Any`.
  entry .Content   a complex type's content model
      `Empty`; `Particles(particle)` for a content model written directly;
      `SimpleExtension(base, attributes)` and `SimpleRestriction(base, facets, attributes)`
      for `simpleContent`; and `ComplexExtension(base, additions, attributes)` and
      `ComplexRestriction(base, particle, attributes)` for `complexContent`.
  entry .ComplexType   a `complexType` declaration, named or anonymous
    entry #name   the type's name, or `Unset` for an anonymous one
    entry #mixed   whether character data may appear among the child elements
    entry #content   the content model
    entry #attributes   the attribute uses declared directly on the type
    entry #anyAttribute   whether an `anyAttribute` wildcard is declared
    entry #documentation   the text of its `annotation/documentation` children
  entry .Import   an `import` of another schema: its namespace and location, each optional
    entry #namespace   the imported namespace, if given
    entry #location   the `schemaLocation`, if given
  entry .Error   raised when a schema document cannot be read
    detail
        Error 992: _the XML Schema could not be read because `reason`_. Raised by `Xsd.parse`
        through its `Tactic[Xsd.Error]`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

        capture[Xsd.Error](Xsd.parse(t"<root/>".read[Xml])).reason
        // Xsd.Error.Reason.NotSchema(Xml.Name(t"root"))
        ```
    entry .Reason   why the schema could not be read
        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `NotSchema(found)`  1  _the document element is `found`, not an XML Schema_  the document element is not `xs:schema`
        row  `Unexpected(element, within)`  2  _`element` may not appear in `within`_  an element the grammar does not allow there
        row  `MissingAttribute(element, attribute)`  3  _the `element` has no `attribute` attribute_
        row  `BadOccurs(value)`  4  _`value` is not a valid number of occurrences_  a `minOccurs` or `maxOccurs` which is neither a number nor `unbounded`
        row  `UnknownPrefix(prefix)`  5  _the prefix `prefix` is not bound to a namespace_  in a QName-valued attribute
        row  `BadFacet(facet, value)`  6  _`value` is not a valid value for the `facet` facet_  a facet whose value is not a number
        row  `Duplicate(kind, name)`  7  _the `kind` `name` is declared more than once_  a global declaration made twice
        ```
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   the `Xsd.Error.Reason`
  entry .parse   reads a schema from an `Xml` document, or from its text
      `Xsd.parse(xml)` reads a parsed document, needing a `Tactic[Xsd.Error]`.
      `Xsd.parse(text)` first parses the text — which may begin with an XML declaration —
      with the freeform vocabulary and strict namespaces, since a schema document binds
      every prefix it uses, and so also needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`.
  entry #targetNamespace   the `targetNamespace`, or `Unset` for a schema with none
  entry #elementFormDefault   whether local elements are qualified by default
  entry #attributeFormDefault   whether local attributes are qualified by default
  entry #elements   the global element declarations, by name, in declaration order
  entry #complexTypes   the named complex types, by name
  entry #simpleTypes   the named simple types, by name
  entry #attributes   the global attribute declarations, by name
  entry #groups   the named model groups, by name, each as its single compositor
  entry #attributeGroups   the named attribute groups, by name
  entry #imports   the schema's `import`s, in order; recorded, not fetched
  entry #includes   the `schemaLocation`s of its `include`s and `redefine`s; recorded, not fetched
  entry #documentation   the text of the schema's own `annotation/documentation` children
  entry #local   whether a resolved name refers into this schema: its namespace is the target namespace
  entry #element   a global element declaration by name, or `Unset`
  entry #resolve   the type a reference names: declared here, or a builtin, or `Unset`
      A name in the target namespace finds a complex type, then a simple type; a name in
      the XSD namespace finds a `Builtin`, wrapped as `SimpleType.Builtin`. Anything else —
      a type from an imported schema, which is not fetched — is `Unset`, and an
      `Xml.Provider` reads such a field as raw `Xml`.
  entry #builtin   the builtin a name in the XSD namespace denotes, or `Unset`

entry .XmlSchema   the vocabulary a parse validates against: which elements, attributes and entities are admitted
  detail
      A parse of XML needs an `XmlSchema` in scope, as it needs a `Codepage` or an error
      strategy; it decides which elements and attributes are admissible where, and which
      named entities expand. `XmlSchema.Freeform`, imported as
      `xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema`, admits any element and attribute and expands the five
      predefined entities, and is the usual choice when no vocabulary is in play. Every
      `Xml` value carries its schema as its `Form` type member, so a document parsed under
      one vocabulary is typed by it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      t"<message>hello &amp; goodbye</message>".read[Xml]
      ```

      A vocabulary of its own is an object extending `XmlSchema` with its `#elements`, as
      `Tag`s, its `#attributes` and its `#entities`; a parse under it rejects an element the
      vocabulary lacks (`Xml.Issue.InvalidTag`) or one which cannot be a child of its parent
      (`Xml.Issue.InadmissibleTag`).
  refer xylophone.xmlSchemas
  refer xylophone.Tag
  entry .generic   a root tag which admits no children, for a schema with no elements
  entry .defaultEntities   the five predefined entities: `amp`, `lt`, `gt`, `apos` and `quot`
  entry .Freeform   the vocabulary which admits anything
      No elements or attributes of its own, the default entities, and `freeform` true, so
      the parser accepts every element and attribute it meets. Imported as the given
      `xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema`.
  entry #freeform   whether any element and attribute is admitted; false unless overridden
  entry #elements   the vocabulary's elements, as `Tag`s by name
  entry #attributes   the vocabulary's attributes, as `Xml.XmlAttribute`s by name
  entry #entities   the named entities and their expansions
  entry #infer   the tag to insert between a parent and a child which cannot be its direct child, or `Unset`
  entry #generic   a root tag admitting every element of the vocabulary as a child

entry .Namespace   a prefix bound to a namespace URI, as a contextual value
  detail
      A `Namespace` binds a prefix, its `Self`, to a URI, its `Topic`, both as singleton
      types, so that a given such as

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given svg: ("svg" is Namespace of "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg") = Namespace()
      ```

      binds `svg` wherever it is in scope. The `x"…"` and `xp"…"` interpolators consult the
      givens for prefixes their literals use but do not declare — `x"<svg:rect/>"` compiles
      with the given above and not without it — and `Xml.Scope[prefixes]`, with the prefixes
      as a tuple type, builds a scope from them, through which a parse or a prefixed
      selection (`` xml.`svg:rect` `` or `xp"//svg:rect"`) matches by resolved name.
      Prefixes are lexical conveniences: two
      names are the same name when their URIs and local parts agree, whatever prefixes
      bind them, so the prefix the code uses need not be the one the document used.

      The reserved `xml` prefix is bound in every document without a declaration, by the
      companion's `xml` given.
  refer xylophone.Xml
  refer xylophone.XPath
  entry .Bound   the class a `Namespace()` makes: a prefix and URI as values, typed by their literals
    entry #prefix   the prefix, as text
    entry #uri   the namespace URI, as text
  entry .apply   `Namespace()`: a binding whose prefix and URI are read from the expected type
      An inline method which takes the prefix and URI from the singleton types of the given
      it is assigned to: `given k: ("k" is Namespace of "urn:a") = Namespace()`.
  entry .xml   the reserved `xml` prefix, bound to `http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace`
      In implicit scope through the companion, so every document and literal has it.
  entry .inspectable   renders a binding as `Namespace[t"svg" -> t"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"]`
  entry #Self   the prefix, as a literal type
  entry #Topic   the namespace URI, as a literal type
  entry #prefix   the prefix, as text
  entry #uri   the namespace URI, as text

entry .Tag   an element of a vocabulary: its name, its preset attributes, and which children it admits
  detail
      A `Tag` is an `Xml.Element` with no children which knows what may go inside it: the
      `#admissible` child names and the `#presets` every instance starts with. Applying it
      with named arguments, `tag(id = t"main")`, is checked by a macro against the
      `Xml.XmlAttribute` givens of the vocabulary — an attribute the element does not take
      is a compile error, _the attribute `foo` cannot be used on the element `<div>`_ — and
      each value is converted through an `Attributive` instance. `object Xml` is itself a
      `Tag.Container`, and an `XmlSchema`'s `elements` are tags.

      The object is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, since honeycomb's `Tag` has the
      name there: reach it as `xylophone.Tag`.
  refer xylophone.XmlSchema
  refer xylophone.Attributive
  entry .root   a document-root tag, `#root`, admitting the given children
      The tag above a vocabulary's elements, which `XmlSchema#generic` builds.
  entry .freeform   a container tag of any name, admitting no particular children
  entry .container   a container tag typed by its label, its admissible children and its schema
      `Tag.container[label, children, schema](presets)` builds a `Container of label over
      children in schema`, the children reified from a type-level list of names.
  entry .Container   a tag whose instances are elements with children
    detail
        Applying a `Container` makes an `Xml.Element` which is also `Xml.Vacuiscible`, so it
        can take its children in a second application: `div(id = t"x")(child, child)`.
    entry #node   the element for a set of attributes, the presets merged beneath them
  entry #presets   the attributes every instance of the tag starts with
  entry #admissible   the names of the elements the tag admits as children
  entry #Result   the type of element applying the tag makes
  entry #applyDynamicNamed   `tag(key = value, …)`: an element with checked attributes
      A macro: each key must be an attribute the vocabulary declares for this element (an
      `Xml.XmlAttribute` given), and each value must be `Attributive` to that attribute's
      type, or the call does not compile.
  entry #node   the element for a set of attributes

entry .Attributive   how a value becomes an attribute's text, when a tag is applied
  detail
      `tag(width = 10)` needs to turn `10` into the text of the `width` attribute, and
      only if the attribute takes a value of that type: `Attributive` is that conversion,
      from a `Self` value to a `(key, value)` pair of texts, or `Unset` to omit the attribute,
      keyed by the attribute's `Result` type. The companion provides `Text` and `String`
      for textual attributes; a vocabulary adds instances for its own attribute types.

      The trait is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, since honeycomb's `Attributive`
      has the name there: reach it as `xylophone.Attributive`.
  refer xylophone.Tag
  entry .Textual   the attribute type of a plain text attribute
  entry .textTextual   a `Text` as a textual attribute's value
  entry .stringTextual   a `String` as a textual attribute's value
  entry #attribute   the `(key, value)` pair for a value, or `Unset` to omit the attribute

entry .Renderable   a value which renders as `Xml` of a given form
  detail
      `Renderable` is the typeclass of types which have an XML rendering: `render` turns a
      `Self` value into an `Xml of Form`, where `Form` names the vocabulary the rendering
      belongs to. It is the XML counterpart of honeycomb's `Renderable` for HTML, and is not
      exported to the `soundness` umbrella because that one has the name: reach it as
      `xylophone.Renderable`.
  entry #render   the value as `Xml of Form`

entry .Inlinable   a compiletime code generator for reading a type from XML, composed into one flat parser
  detail
      `Inlinable` is the `Expr`-level counterpart of `Xml.Parsable`: a typeclass whose
      `#parse` is a macro-time generator, given an `Expr` of an `Xml.Reader` positioned
      just inside an opened element and returning an `Expr` of the decoded value, which the
      generating macro splices straight into the parser it builds. Nested records,
      collection gathering and custom leaf parsers all inline into one monomorphic parser
      with no runtime dispatch, no instance arrays and no adapter hops between composed
      parsers. It follows jacinta's `Inlinable` for JSON exactly.

      The entry points are `Inlinable.parsable[T]`, a monomorphic `T is Xml.Parsable`, and
      `Inlinable.printable[T]`, its writing counterpart, a `T is Xml.Printable` which
      writes a record's compact XML text straight into a writer with no tree built. Both
      are what `import codecs.direct` and `derives Xml.Direct` reach for.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      case class Tree(label: Text, children: List[Tree])
      given Tree is Xml.Parsable = Inlinable.parsable[Tree]
      given Tree is Xml.Printable = Inlinable.printable[Tree]

      val tree = Tree(t"root", List(Tree(t"leaf", Nil)))
      tree.in[Text over Xml].read[Tree in Xml] == tree
      ```

      Instances are ordinary runtime values, so `derived` needs no macro of its own, and
      the resolution ladder at a `parsable[T]` expansion is: a builtin leaf (the
      companion's `int`, `text`, … givens, which mirror the primitive `Xml.Parsable`
      instances, so direct, staged and inlined reads give equal values and equal errors),
      then a user-written instance evaluated live inside an in-macro staging compiler,
      then a structural generator for a case class, a collection or a sealed sum, and
      finally the runtime seam, a spliced call through the field's `Xml.Parsing` instance.
      A recursive occurrence (`Tree` above) takes the seam rather than expanding forever.

      The staging step needs the `xylophone.staged` module on the macro classpath, which
      supplies the `Inlinable.Stager`; without it, a field whose type has only a
      user-written instance takes the runtime seam. An instance (and its type) must also be
      compiled in an earlier run than the expansion: a same-run instance degrades to the
      seam through `Xml.Field`.
  refer xylophone.Xml
  refer xylophone.inlining
  entry .parsable   `Inlinable.parsable[T]`: a monomorphic `T is Xml.Parsable`, generated at compiletime
      Like `Xml.Parsable.staged`, but composed through `Inlinable` instances, so that nested
      records, collections and custom leaves inline into one flat parser. The usual way to
      get one is `import codecs.direct` or `case class T(…) derives Xml.Direct`.
  entry .printable   `Inlinable.printable[T]`: a monomorphic `T is Xml.Printable`, generated at compiletime
      Writes a record's compact XML text straight into a writer, reached through
      `in[Text over Xml]` and `in[Data over Xml]` where the formatting is compact; indented
      formatting still renders through the tree.
  entry .Generates   evidence that the generator builds a type's codec structurally, as a record
    detail
        The condition on `import codecs.direct`'s generated instances: its `generates` given
        succeeds only for a type the generator handles as a record, so for any other type
        the search for a generated instance fails and the derived path resolves instead.
    entry .generates   the given, a macro which succeeds for a record type and fails otherwise
  entry .Stager   the in-macro staging compiler which evaluates a user-written instance at expansion
    detail
        An `Inlinable` given is an ordinary runtime value, so the generator can only call its
        `parse` by evaluating the given inside the macro, which takes a staging compiler and
        with it the compiler jars that `xylophone.core` does not depend on. The
        `xylophone.staged` module holds the implementation, which the companion loads by
        name when it is on the macro classpath; without it, `summon` is `None` and such a
        field takes the runtime seam.
    entry .summon   the live instance for a field type, if the staging compiler is available and finds one
    entry .discriminantAttribute   the attribute a sealed sum's `Discriminable in Xml` dispatches on, if any
    entry #summon   the live instance for a field type, or `None`
    entry #discriminantAttribute   the sum's discriminant attribute, or `None`
  entry .derived   the structural instance for a case class
      Reflects `Self` when `parse` is invoked, so no macro is needed: `Type[Self]` arrives
      with the call.
  entry .ForXml   the `derives`-clause carrier: `case class Foo(…) derives Inlinable.ForXml`
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass has no type parameters, so it cannot appear in a `derives`
        clause; `ForXml[value]` is a parameterized subtrait which *is* a `value is
        Inlinable`, synthesized into the type's companion, where the staging summon finds it.
        The resolution ladder unwraps the delegate, so structural instances keep their
        generator identities.
    entry .derived   the carrier over `Inlinable.derived[value]`
    entry #parse   delegates to the wrapped instance
    entry #absent   delegates to the wrapped instance
  entry .int   the generator for `Int`: the same read as `Xml.intParsable`, with `0` when absent
  entry .long   the generator for `Long`
  entry .double   the generator for `Double`
  entry .float   the generator for `Float`
  entry .boolean   the generator for `Boolean`
  entry .text   the generator for `Text`: the element's text, or `Xml.Error.Reason.Untextual`
  entry .string   the generator for `String`
  entry .iterable   the generator for any `Iterable` of an inlinable element type
      In field position the record generator never calls it: it gathers each same-named
      child through the element's own generator. On its own it reads a single element as a
      one-element collection, as the runtime `Xml.Parsable.iterable` does.
  entry #parse   the generator: an `Expr` of the reader to an `Expr` of the value
      The reader is positioned with the current element just opened. The `Quotes` and the
      `Type[Self]` arrive with the call.
  entry #absent   what a field of this type yields when no child carries its name
      Mirrors the runtime instances: an abort with `Xml.Error.Reason.Missing` unless
      overridden; the primitive instances raise and continue with a sentinel, a collection
      is empty, and a record raises once and takes a `Default` or builds its fields absent.

entry .inlining   the machinery behind `Inlinable`: the generators and entry macros
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the expansion-time instance resolution, the structural generators
      for products, collections and sums, and the macros `Inlinable.parsable`,
      `Inlinable.printable` and `Inlinable.Generates` expand to.
  entry .fieldInstance   the runtime seam: a field type's `Xml.Parsing`, a nominal `Parsable` in scope first
      An inline method the generated record body binds once per parse, preferring a
      nominal `Xml.Parsable` (a custom instance, a staged sibling) to the `Xml.Field`
      fallback chain, so that occurrence reads, repeatability and absence consult one
      instance.
  entry .generates   the macro behind `Inlinable.Generates.generates`
  entry .inlinablePrintable   the macro behind `Inlinable.printable[T]`
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `Inlinable.parsable[T]`

entry .dynamicAccess   the permission for the dynamic syntax `xml.foo`, as a given to import by name
  detail
      Member-style navigation of an `Xml` value — `xml.book`, `xml.book(Prim)`,
      `` xml.`svg:rect` `` — is unlocked by an erased `Xml is Dynamical` permission, so that
      a scope which has not asked for it keeps its mistyped member names as compile
      errors. The permission is granted to the rest of a scope by importing the given, or to
      a single block by `dynamically[Xml]:` (or `dynamically:`, for every format).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml

      val data = t"<a><b><c>42</c></b></a>".read[Xml]
      data.b().c().as[Int]   // 42
      ```
  entry .dynamicXml   grants `Xml is Dynamical` to the scope which imports it

entry .internal   the macros behind `x"…"`, `xp"…"`, the XML extractor, tag application and staged parsers
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object which houses xylophone's macro implementations. It also
      declares the opaque types `Attributes` and `Scope`, which the `xylophone` package
      exports (and `object Xml` re-exports `Scope`, as `Xml.Scope`, since the `soundness`
      umbrella already has another `Scope`); their pages under the package have the full
      account.
  entry .extractor   the macro behind the `x"…"` pattern: matches an `Xml` against a literal with holes
      A literal in pattern position captures each hole's value: a single captured node is
      the result, several are a tuple, and a single text value alone cannot be captured on
      its own (capture it with another value, or the enclosing node).
  entry .xpath   the macro behind `xp"…"`: parses, checks and lifts the expression
      Parses the literal at expansion time with the same parser the runtime decoder uses,
      maps a failure's offset back to a source position, grafts the insertions in place of
      their holes, lifts the tree wholesale, and binds the prefixes the expression uses
      through the `Namespace` givens at the call site.
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind `x"…"`: parses and checks the literal, with holes for insertions
      Parses leniently, so a literal may use a prefix bound only by a `Namespace` given at
      its call site, and then checks every prefix against the givens itself; an unbound
      prefix is a compile error unless `namespaceOptions.lenientNamespaces` is in scope.
  entry .attributes   the macro behind `tag(key = value, …)`: checks each attribute and converts its value
      Each key must be an `Xml.XmlAttribute` given for the tag's element, and each value
      `Attributive` to the attribute's type; otherwise the call does not compile.
  entry .stagedParsable   the macro behind `Xml.Parsable.staged`: a monomorphic parser for a case class
      Field values live in typed locals, child elements dispatch through packed-`Long`
      literal comparisons, builtin primitives read through direct static calls, `@attribute`
      fields fill from the open tag, and the record is built by a direct constructor call,
      with the same semantics as the derived engine.
  entry .Attributes   an element's attributes, in document order; see `xylophone.Attributes`
    detail
        The opaque type over a frozen interleaved array, exported to the `xylophone` package,
        whose page has the full account.
    entry .empty   no attributes
    entry .traversable   the pairs in document order, so `attributes.to[List]` needs no conversion
    entry .inspectable   renders the attributes as `xml{id="x", class="y"}`
    entry .indexable   the `Applicable` given: `attributes.at(key)` as an `Optional`, and `contains`
    entry .apply   `Attributes(pairs*)` builds from pairs; `attributes(key)` is a value, throwing if absent
        The constructor keeps the pairs' order. The lookup throws `NoSuchElementException`
        for a key which is not present; `fetch` or `at` give `Unset` instead.
    entry .from   builds from a `Map[Text, Text]`, in the map's iteration order
    entry .size   the number of attributes
    entry .isEmpty   whether there are no attributes
    entry .nonEmpty   whether there is at least one attribute
    entry .nil   whether there are no attributes
    entry .fetch   the value for a key, or `Unset`
        The lookup the staged parsers' `@attribute` steps use: `at` without the `Applicable`
        detour.
    entry .declaresNamespace   whether any attribute is an `xmlns` or `xmlns:prefix` declaration
    entry .contains   whether a key is present
    entry .keys   the keys, in document order
    entry .values   the values, in document order
    entry .iterator   the `(key, value)` pairs, in document order
    entry .toMap   the attributes as a `Map[Text, Text]` which still iterates in document order
    entry .eachPair   applies an action to each `(key, value)` pair, in order
        Named `eachPair` rather than `each`: the generic one-parameter `each` would otherwise
        win resolution and reject the two-parameter lambda.
    entry .foreachPair   `eachPair` as a plain, non-inline method
    entry .map   maps each `(key, value)` pair to a value, in order
    entry .removed   the attributes without a key; the same value if the key is absent
    entry .-   `attributes - key`, the same as `removed`
    entry .--   the attributes without each of several keys
    entry .updated   the attributes with a key set to a value, in place if present, appended if not
    entry .++   the attributes merged with another `Attributes` or a `Map`, the right side winning
        Keys of the left keep their positions, with the right's values where both have a key,
        and the right's new keys follow in its order.
    entry .equalsAttributes   whether two attribute sets hold the same pairs, in any order
    entry .hashAttributes   an order-independent hash, consistent with `equalsAttributes`
  entry .Scope   the namespace bindings in force at an element; see `xylophone.Scope`
    detail
        The opaque type over a frozen interleaved array, exported to the `xylophone` package
        and re-exported as `Xml.Scope`, whose page has the full account.
    entry .xmlNamespace   `http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace`, the URI of the reserved `xml` prefix
    entry .xmlnsNamespace   `http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/`, the URI of the `xmlns` prefix
    entry .empty   no bindings at all, not even `xml`
    entry .xml   the reserved `xml` prefix alone: the scope a document is parsed in unless another is given
    entry .default   the given scope, `xml`, in implicit scope through the companion
    entry .inspectable   renders a scope as `xmlns{"" ="urn:d", p="urn:p"}`
    entry .apply   `Scope(pairs*)` from prefix–URI pairs; `Scope[prefixes]` from `Namespace` givens
        `Xml.Scope(t"svg" -> t"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg")` binds the pairs in order, the
        empty text as the prefix of the default namespace. The type-level form takes a tuple
        type of prefixes and binds each through the `Namespace` given for it, over the
        reserved `xml` binding.
    entry .isEmpty   whether the scope has no bindings
    entry .size   the number of bindings
    entry .resolve   the URI bound to a prefix, `Unset` for the default namespace; `Unset` if unbound or undeclared
    entry .binds   whether a prefix has a binding, counting an undeclaring one
    entry .prefixOf   the most recently bound prefix for a URI, or `Unset` if none binds it
        The result is doubly optional: `Optional(Unset)` means the URI is the default
        namespace, and `Unset` that no prefix is bound to it. The serializer uses it to write
        a name in a namespace.
    entry .bind   the scope with one more binding appended, shadowing any earlier one of the prefix
    entry .++   this scope with another's bindings appended, so the other's shadow this one's
    entry .bindings   the bindings in order, as `(prefix, uri)` pairs, `Unset` for the default namespace

entry .optics   lenses and optics over an element's children, for `xml.lens(…)`
  detail
      The givens which let an `Xml` value be updated through a lens: `xml.lens(_.book.title =
      value)` navigates to a child element by name and replaces it, and the `Each` and
      ordinal optics address every, or the n-th, child element. Every update rebuilds the
      element immutably, preserving text and comment nodes in place, and the value assigned
      need not be XML already: anything `Encodable in Xml` is encoded as it is assigned.
      The givens are in a choice package, imported by name, and need `dynamicAccess.dynamicXml`
      for the member syntax inside the lens.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml
      import optics.{xmlLens, xmlEachOptical, xmlOrdinalOptical}

      val document = t"<doc><x>1</x><x>2</x><x>3</x></doc>".read[Xml]
      document.lens(_.x = x"<x>9</x>").show        // the first <x> replaced
      document.lens(_(Each) = x"<x>0</x>").show    // every <x> replaced
      document.lens(_(Sec) = x"<x>0</x>").show     // the second <x> replaced
      ```
  entry .xmlLens   a lens from an element to its first child element of a name, replacing it on update
      Navigates to the first child element with the given name and replaces it on update,
      or appends the new value if no such child exists. Needs an `Xml.Scope` in scope (the
      companion's default binds only `xml`) and the `Xml is Dynamical` permission.
  entry .xmlOrdinalOptical   an optic to the n-th child element: `_(Sec) = …`
      Counts element children only, so text and comments between them do not shift the
      ordinal.
  entry .xmlEachOptical   an optic to every child element: `_(Each) = …`

entry .optionalityOptions   how an `Optional` field reads a missing or faulty element or attribute
  detail
      Two policies for an `Optional` field of a derived decoder, each a given to import by
      name. *Absence* — no element or attribute of the field's name — is lenient by default,
      giving `Unset`; `strictXmlAbsence` makes it an `Xml.Error` with `Reason.Missing`.
      *Faults* — a present element the inner decoder rejects — are strict by default,
      raising; `lenientXmlFaults` reads such a field as `Unset`. XML has no null: an empty
      element is a present value, so there is no nullity policy, where JSON and YAML have one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      case class Tally(name: Text, count: Optional[Int])

      val tolerant =
        import optionalityOptions.lenientXmlFaults
        x"<root><name>Ann</name><count>many</count></root>".as[Tally]   // Tally(t"Ann", Unset)
      ```
  entry .strictXmlAbsence   a missing element or attribute for an `Optional` field is an `Xml.Error`
  entry .lenientXmlAbsence   a missing element or attribute for an `Optional` field is `Unset`; the default
  entry .strictXmlFaults   a present element the inner decoder rejects raises; the default
  entry .lenientXmlFaults   a present element the inner decoder rejects reads as `Unset`

entry .namespaceOptions   whether a prefix with no binding is an error, or no namespace
  detail
      The Namespaces recommendation makes an unbound prefix an error, and that is the
      default: parsing `<p:a/>` with no declaration of `p` raises a `Parse.Error` with
      `Xml.Issue.UnboundPrefix`, and `x"<p:a/>"` with no `Namespace` given for `p` does not
      compile. `lenientNamespaces` has an unbound prefix resolve to no namespace instead,
      for documents which use prefixes they never declare.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import namespaceOptions.lenientNamespaces

      t"<p:a/>".read[Xml]
      ```
  entry .strictNamespaces   an unbound prefix is an error; the default
  entry .lenientNamespaces   an unbound prefix resolves to no namespace

entry .xmlSchemas   the vocabulary a parse validates against, as a given to import by name
  detail
      Every parse of XML needs an `XmlSchema` in scope. `freeformXmlSchema` is
      `XmlSchema.Freeform`, which admits any element and attribute and the five predefined
      entities, and is the usual choice when no vocabulary is in play.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      t"<message>hello</message>".read[Xml]
      ```
  entry .freeformXmlSchema   `XmlSchema.Freeform`: any element, any attribute, the predefined entities

entry .formatting   how XML is laid out when it is rendered, as a given to import by name
  detail
      An `Xml.Formatting` decides whether `show`, `in[Text over Xml]` and `Xml.emit` write
      whitespace between elements. Compact formatting writes none; indented formatting
      breaks each element onto its own line, indented two spaces per level, and ends with
      a newline. A record with an `Xml.Printable` is written directly, with no tree built,
      only where the formatting is compact.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema

      case class Worker(name: Text, age: Int)

      locally:
        import formatting.indentedXmlFormatting
        Worker(t"Alice", 30).in[Xml].show   // across several lines
      ```
  entry .compactXmlFormatting   no whitespace between elements, and no trailing newline
  entry .indentedXmlFormatting   each element on its own line, indented two spaces per level, with a trailing newline

entry .Attributes   an element's attributes: an ordered, immutable map from name to value
  detail
      The attributes of an `Xml.Element`, in document order, as an opaque type over a
      frozen array of interleaved keys and values rather than a `Map`: a parse builds one
      as it tokenizes the start tag, an element's `attributes` is one, and every update
      (`updated`, `removed`, `++`) returns a new value. It is declared in
      `xylophone.internal` and exported to the `xylophone` package.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val attributes: Attributes = Attributes(t"id" -> t"x", t"class" -> t"y")
      attributes(t"id")                  // t"x"; throws if absent
      attributes.fetch(t"missing")       // Unset
      attributes.at(t"class")            // Optional, through `Applicable`
      attributes.updated(t"id", t"z").toMap
      Xml.Element(t"a", attributes, Array())
      ```

      Equality of two `Attributes` is order-insensitive (`equalsAttributes`), which is what
      `Xml.Element`'s own equality uses; iteration (`keys`, `values`, `iterator`,
      `eachPair`, `to[List]`) is in document order. It inspects as
      `xml{id="x", class="y"}`, as the attributes would appear in a start tag.
  refer xylophone.Xml
  refer xylophone.Scope

entry .Scope   the namespace bindings in force at an element: prefixes paired with URIs
  detail
      A `Scope` is the list of `xmlns` and `xmlns:prefix` declarations which apply at a
      point in a document, as prefix–URI pairs in order, a later binding of a prefix
      shadowing an earlier one; the default namespace is the binding of the empty prefix,
      and an undeclaring `xmlns=""` binds it to the empty URI. Every `Xml.Element` carries
      its scope, the parser extending a parent's with each child's declarations, and a
      path's `XPath#scope` binds the prefixes its name tests use. It is declared in
      `xylophone.internal`, exported to the `xylophone` package, and reached as `Xml.Scope`
      from the `soundness` umbrella, which already has another `Scope`.

      A `Scope` given in scope decides how a prefixed selection resolves, whatever prefix
      the document itself used:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import xmlSchemas.freeformXmlSchema
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicXml

      val doc = t"""<r xmlns:a="urn:a"><a:x>1</a:x><b:x xmlns:b="urn:b">2</b:x></r>""".read[Xml]

      locally:
        given Xml.Scope = Xml.Scope(t"q" -> t"urn:b")
        doc.`q:x`().as[Int]   // 2: the <b:x>, matched by namespace
      ```

      The companion's `default` given, in implicit scope, is `xml`: only the reserved `xml`
      prefix, which every document has without a declaration. A scope takes no part in an
      element's equality: a parsed element equals the same element built by hand, and when a
      tree is written, an element whose scope binds a prefix it uses which nothing above it
      has declared gets the declaration written for it.
  refer xylophone.Namespace
  refer xylophone.XPath
